#!/usr/bin/env node
// Copyright 2026 Lee James
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

// Realistic conversations for the Audit Log: longer prompts, and threads with
// several prompts rather than one.
//
// Runs after the other sample scripts (see the order in load.cjs), after the seeders that write the threads. For
// every seeded thread (ff-, ff2-, ff3-, ff4-, ff5-, ff8- rows) whose opening
// line it recognises, it:
//   - gives the opener a full, multi-paragraph version (preview unchanged),
//   - rewrites the thread's later prompts as on-topic follow-ups,
//   - adds follow-up turns, so threads run 2 to 7 prompts,
//   - copies the opener's classification onto added Cowork prompts, so the
//     classifier never spends a call on them.
// Each seeded flagged prompt (ff6-) also gets two lead-in turns, so an alert's
// "View prompt in Audit Log" opens a conversation, not a lone line.
//
// Added rows carry the marker ff13-. Re-running clears and re-adds them. The
// rewritten seeded rows are recreated by their own seeders at the next sample load.
// The text holds no personal data, real names or real brands (checked with
// lib/pii/detect.ts and lib/intent/detect.ts when written).
// Usage: docker exec -i ccc-hub node < 10-conversations.cjs   [--clean]

const DATA = {"threads":[{"match":"Review this PR for race conditions in the retry logic.","surface":"cowork","full":"Review this PR for race conditions in the retry logic. It changes how the order sync worker retries failed calls to the warehouse service. Previously each failure was retried inline; now failures go onto an in-memory queue and a background loop drains it every 5 seconds with exponential backoff, capped at 6 attempts.\n\nWhat worries me is that two workers can run on the same node during a rolling deploy, and the queue is keyed by order reference while the attempt counter lives on a separate map. Please look specifically at the read-then-write on the attempt counter, the point where we mark an order as synced, and whether a late success can overwrite a newer failure.\n\nIgnore style comments for now, I only care about correctness.","followUps":["Good catch on the counter. Can you show the smallest change that makes the increment atomic without pulling in a lock library?","You assumed the queue is shared across workers but it isn't, each worker process has its own. The shared state is the database row. Here's the statement that updates it:\n\nUPDATE order_sync SET status = 'synced', attempts = attempts + 1 WHERE order_ref = :ref\n\nThere's no check on the current status before the write, so if worker A succeeds after worker B has already recorded a newer failure, A wins. Can you redo the analysis with that in mind and tell me whether a conditional update on a version column is enough on its own?","Follow-up on shutdown. Our deploys give each pod 30 seconds after SIGTERM before it's killed, and the drain loop sleeps for 5 seconds between passes, so in theory there's time to flush. But the handler we register only closes the HTTP server, it never signals the retry loop. I think anything in the queue at that moment is simply lost and the order stays failed until the nightly reconciliation picks it up, which can be up to 20 hours later. Can you confirm that reading of the code and suggest how to make the loop drain and exit cleanly?","OK. Write this up as a review comment I can paste on the PR, grouped as must fix, should fix and nice to have. Keep it kind, the author is fairly new.","Also add a suggested test that reproduces the overwrite with two concurrent workers. Pseudocode is fine."]},{"match":"Draft an ADR for the new ingest pipeline.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft an ADR for the new ingest pipeline. We are replacing the nightly batch import with a streaming pipeline: producers push events to a queue, a consumer service validates and enriches them, and writes land in the warehouse within about two minutes instead of the next morning.\n\nUse our usual ADR format: context, decision, options considered, consequences and status. Options we looked at were (1) keep the batch job and run it hourly, (2) a managed streaming service, (3) build on the message queue we already run for notifications. We chose option 3, mainly on cost and because the platform team already supports it.\n\nThe audience is the architecture review group, so be precise about trade-offs and don't oversell it.","followUps":["the consequences section is too rosy. add the operational cost: someone has to own the consumer on call","couple of corrections. option 2 wasn't rejected on cost alone, the bigger problem was data residency, the managed service only offered a region outside the UK at the tier we could afford. and option 1 was never really viable because hourly runs would overlap when month-end volumes spike, last march a single batch took 74 minutes. please fix the options section so the reasoning matches what actually happened","add a short section on how we roll back if the consumer falls behind","status should be Proposed, not Accepted. review is on thursday","also want a migration plan as an appendix. rough shape: run both pipelines in parallel for two weeks, compare row counts and totals daily, move reporting over once we've had five clean days in a row, then switch off the batch job but keep the code for a month. flag that finance reporting is the consumer most sensitive to late data, they close the month on working day 3"]},{"match":"Summarise this week's incident postmortem into action items.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise this week's incident postmortem into action items. The incident was a 47 minute outage of the customer portal on Tuesday morning, caused by a certificate renewal that succeeded on the load balancer but not on two of the backend nodes. Health checks kept passing because they hit the nodes over plain HTTP internally.\n\nThe postmortem doc is long and rambling, so I've pasted the timeline and discussion sections below. I need a clean list of action items with an owner, a priority and a rough size for each, so I can drop them into the backlog before the Friday review.\n\nTimeline excerpt: 08:12 first customer report, 08:20 on-call paged, 08:41 root cause suspected, 08:59 manual renewal on both nodes, 09:00 recovery confirmed.","followUps":["Merge items 2 and 5, they're the same fix.","Owners should be teams, not people. Use platform, SRE and the portal team.","You missed the alerting gap. From the discussion section: 'we only knew because a customer contacted support, our synthetic checks run from inside the network so they never saw the expired cert.' That's arguably the most important action. Add it as P1 with SRE as owner, and add a second item to review every other service where the health check doesn't follow the same path as real traffic. Also the timeline shows 8 minutes between first report and paging, which nobody discussed, so add a smaller item to look at the handoff from support to on-call.","Sizes look high to me. Certificate expiry monitoring is a day, not a week, because the platform team already has a check for the load balancer and it just needs pointing at the backend nodes as well. The health check change is bigger than you've said though, since every service owner has to agree the new path and some of them will need a deploy. Recheck the sizes on the others with that in mind, and mark anything you're unsure about as needing an estimate from the owning team rather than guessing.","Now give me a three line summary for the engineering channel. No blame, just what happened and what we're doing about it."]},{"match":"Write the runbook for the deploy rollback procedure.","surface":"cowork","full":"Write the runbook for the deploy rollback procedure. Right now the knowledge lives with two senior engineers and the on-call rota has grown to nine people, several of whom have never done a rollback in production.\n\nOur setup: services deploy through the CI pipeline as container images tagged with the build number, the orchestrator keeps the last five revisions, and database migrations run as a separate job before the new version starts. Most rollbacks are just pointing the service back at the previous revision. The hard case is when a migration has already run.\n\nWrite it for someone tired at 2am. Numbered steps, a decision point at the top, and clear stop points where they should escalate.","followUps":["Step 4 says to 'check the dashboard'. Which one? Say the service error rate panel.","Important correction on migrations. We don't write down migrations at all, our policy is expand and contract, so a release should never include a destructive change. In practice that means rolling back the app is safe even after a migration ran, as long as the release followed the policy. The risky case is a release that broke it. So the decision point should be 'was this release flagged as containing a contract step', and if yes, stop and escalate to the database owner. Please restructure around that.","Add a verification section at the end: what good looks like after rollback, and how long to watch it.","add a comms step. post in the incident channel when you start and when you finish","One more thing from the last real rollback. The orchestrator only keeps five revisions, and on a busy day we've shipped more than that, so the revision you want might already be gone. In that case the fix is to redeploy the older image tag through the pipeline using the emergency redeploy option, which a lead has to approve. Add that as a fallback path, and note that it takes about 12 minutes compared with under a minute for a normal rollback."]},{"match":"Turn these customer interview notes into a product spec.","surface":"cowork","full":"Turn these customer interview notes into a product spec. We spoke to eight operations managers at mid-sized logistics firms about how they handle delivery exceptions. The recurring theme was that they find out about failed deliveries from unhappy customers rather than from our platform.\n\nNotes, lightly cleaned up:\n- 6 of 8 check exceptions manually each morning in a spreadsheet export\n- 'I'd pay for something that tells me before the customer does'\n- two want text alerts, most want it in the tool they already use\n- Halden Logistics said their drivers mark failures inconsistently, so any alert needs a reason code\n\nSpec format: problem, users, goals, non-goals, requirements, open questions. Keep requirements testable.","followUps":["Non-goals: add that we're not changing the driver app in this release.","too much detail in requirements. keep it to what, not how, engineering will push back otherwise","I found two more interviews in the shared folder that I hadn't included. Both are larger firms, around 400 vehicles each. Their view was quite different: they already get exception alerts from their telematics provider and the gap for them is grouping, they get hundreds of alerts a day and can't tell which ones matter. One said 'alerts are noise unless they're ranked by customer value'. Please add a section on segment differences and make ranking an explicit open question rather than assuming small firms are the only target.","What success metrics would you suggest? Something we can measure within a quarter of launch.","On metrics: we can't measure time to detection directly today because we don't record when an ops manager first opens an exception. We can measure the share of exceptions acknowledged within 2 hours, and we can count inbound support contacts tagged 'where is my delivery'. Use those two, add a baseline column marked TBC, and drop the NPS idea, the sample per customer is too small to move it."]},{"match":"Draft the Now/Next/Later roadmap for next quarter.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the Now/Next/Later roadmap for next quarter. This is for the product review with the CPO and the heads of engineering and sales. We have three squads and roughly the same capacity as this quarter.\n\nCandidate items: bulk editing for price lists (top sales ask, 14 deals cite it), audit history on records (needed for two enterprise renewals), the mobile approvals flow (half built, paused in July), self-serve single sign-on setup, performance work on the reports page (p95 load is 9 seconds), and a new integrations marketplace that the CEO keeps mentioning.\n\nI want Now to be honest about capacity, not a wish list. Give a one-line rationale for each placement.","followUps":["Integrations marketplace can't sit in Later with no comment, the CEO will ask. Add a line on what we'd need to learn first.","Some context you didn't have. The two enterprise renewals that need audit history are worth about £380k combined and both renew in month two of the quarter, so audit history has to land in the first six weeks, not just somewhere in Now. Meanwhile engineering says the mobile approvals work is closer to done than I thought, about three weeks for one squad. Can you rework Now so audit history and mobile approvals run in parallel and bulk editing starts in the second half?","Performance on reports: move to Now. 9 seconds is losing us demos.","If audit history, mobile approvals and reports performance are all in Now, that uses all three squads for most of the quarter. Bulk editing is the obvious casualty, but sales will push hard because it's cited in 14 deals. Draft a short paragraph I can say out loud explaining why it moves to Next, what we'll do in the meantime (the CSV import workaround support already uses), and when they can expect it. Firm but not defensive.","Format it as a table with columns for item, horizon, squad and rationale. I'll paste it into the deck."]},{"match":"Write acceptance criteria for the new dashboard feature.","surface":"cowork","full":"Write acceptance criteria for the new dashboard feature. The feature lets account managers pin up to six widgets to a personal dashboard, rearrange them by drag and drop, and choose a date range that applies to every widget at once.\n\nWe write criteria in Given/When/Then. The squad is new to the codebase so I'd rather be explicit about edge cases: what happens with no data, what happens when a widget fails to load, and whether the layout persists across devices (it should, it's saved to the user profile).\n\nAccessibility matters here. The keyboard path for reordering has to work, not just drag and drop.","followUps":["Add a criterion for the seventh widget. What does the user see when they try to pin it?","The date range shouldn't apply to the live alerts widget, it's always real time. Carve that out.","QA came back with questions. First, what does 'persists across devices' mean if someone has the dashboard open in two tabs and rearranges in both? Last write wins is fine but we should say so. Second, if a widget is removed from the product later, what happens to dashboards that pinned it? I think it should disappear quietly with a one-time notice. Third, the empty state: design wants a prompt to pin a first widget, not a blank grid. Please add criteria for all three.","can you split these into must have for launch and can follow later","Keyboard reordering needs more detail. Design's spec says: focus a widget, press space to pick it up, arrow keys to move it, space again to drop, escape to cancel and return it to where it started. Screen readers should announce the new position, for example 'moved to position 3 of 5'. Rewrite that criterion with those exact behaviours so QA can test it without guessing."]},{"match":"Analyse feature adoption from last month's metrics.","surface":"cowork","full":"Analyse feature adoption from last month's metrics. We launched saved filters and scheduled exports at the start of the month and I need to tell the product leadership group whether they're landing.\n\nNumbers from the analytics export:\nsaved filters: 2,140 weekly active accounts eligible, 612 created at least one filter, 388 used one more than once\nscheduled exports: 2,140 eligible, 171 created a schedule, 143 still active at month end\nprevious launch (bulk tagging) hit 34% creation in its first month\n\nI want a view on whether these are good or bad, what's driving it, and what we'd look at next. Don't overclaim from one month.","followUps":["Repeat use is the number I care about. Lead with 388 of 612, not the creation rate.","Some context on scheduled exports. It's only available on the Professional plan and above, which is about 640 of the 2,140 accounts, not all of them. So 171 is closer to 27% of eligible accounts, which changes the story quite a lot. Can you redo the comparison with the right denominator, and note that bulk tagging was available on every plan so the 34% isn't like for like either?","What's a sensible target for month two? Give me a range and say what it's based on.","Drop the funnel chart idea, leadership hates them.","Here's the support data too, it might explain the filter drop-off. In the month we had 46 tickets mentioning saved filters. Roughly half were people who couldn't find their saved filters after creating them, because the panel collapses by default. About a dozen asked to share filters with teammates, which we don't support. A handful were bugs with date filters. Fold this in as a likely driver and suggest one quick fix and one bigger bet."]},{"match":"Turn this runbook into a training module.","surface":"cowork","full":"Turn this runbook into a training module. The runbook covers how our support leads handle a priority one customer escalation: triage, who to page, the customer update cadence and the handover to engineering. It's accurate but it reads like a checklist and new team leads don't retain it.\n\nI want a 45 minute module: learning objectives, a short explainer for each stage, one worked scenario, and a five question knowledge check at the end. Audience is newly promoted team leads with six to twelve months in support.\n\nKeep the language plain. Several of the team work in English as a second language.","followUps":["the scenario is too easy. make the customer push back on the update cadence","Add a 'common mistakes' box to each stage. Three bullets max.","Feedback from the senior lead who reviewed it. The section on paging engineering is wrong for out of hours: after 7pm the first page goes to the on-call duty manager, not straight to engineering, and the duty manager decides whether to wake an engineer. She also said the module doesn't mention the internal status note, which leads must update every 30 minutes even when there's nothing new. Please correct both and add a question on the out of hours route to the quiz.","knowledge check answers need explanations, not just the right letter","Can you also produce a facilitator version? Same content but with timings for each section adding up to 45 minutes, prompts for discussion after the scenario, and a note on where people usually get confused (from the senior lead: most new leads over-communicate with the customer in the first hour and under-communicate in the third). Keep the participant version as it is."]},{"match":"Draft the FAQ for the new rollout.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the FAQ for the new rollout. We're moving all staff from the old expenses tool to the expenses module in our finance system from the first of next month. Around 900 people are affected, mostly field sales and consultants who submit weekly.\n\nKey facts: claims already submitted in the old tool will be processed there, anything not submitted by the 28th has to be re-entered in the new one, receipts are captured by photo in the mobile app, and approval routing follows the cost centre manager as before. The mileage rate is unchanged.\n\nWrite 12 to 15 questions in the order people will actually ask them. Friendly, not corporate.","followUps":["Lead with 'what do I need to do before the 28th'. That's what everyone will open it for.","Finance have clarified a few things. Approvals in the new tool go to the line manager, not the cost centre manager, which is a change from the old process, so we need a question that says that plainly. Also, corporate card transactions will import automatically, so people only need to add a receipt and a reason, they shouldn't create a claim from scratch. And there's a one-off grace period: claims older than 60 days are normally rejected, but anything from the old tool re-entered in the first month will be accepted. Please update and make sure nothing contradicts these.","Too long. Cut to 10 questions and merge the mobile ones.","Add a 'where to get help' answer pointing to the finance helpdesk channel and the drop-in sessions on Tuesdays and Thursdays.","Field sales reviewed it and asked two things we haven't covered: what happens if they're travelling with no signal when they photograph a receipt (the app queues it and uploads later, but it has to be opened once while online), and whether they can delegate claim entry to an assistant. Delegation is supported but has to be set up by the claimant in their profile first. Add both, and keep the tone the same."]},{"match":"Summarise the top support tickets this week.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise the top support tickets this week. I've pasted the export from the helpdesk, 312 tickets in total, grouped by tag. I need a short summary for the Monday enablement stand-up: what's driving volume, anything new, and anything that should go to product.\n\nTop tags: sign-in problems 64, report export errors 51, billing questions 38, invite messages not arriving 33, slow dashboard 29, everything else under 20 each. Last week sign-in problems were 22, so that's the obvious spike. The release on Wednesday changed the sign-in page.\n\nKeep it under 200 words.","followUps":["Pull out the sign-in spike as its own line at the top. Everything else can be bullets.","I went through a sample of the sign-in tickets. Most of them are people at Brightwater Retail who use single sign-on. After Wednesday's release they land on the new page and fill in the standard form instead of clicking the 'sign in with your organisation' button, which is now further down. A smaller group have a genuine bug where the page loops on older browsers. Please split those two causes in the summary, the first is a design issue for product and the second is a bug for engineering.","Invite messages: 33 is the highest it's been. Say that, with last week's number (19).","For the product note: include that 41 of the 64 sign-in tickets came from organisations using single sign-on, that the button moved below the fold on laptop screens in Wednesday's release, and that agents resolve each ticket in about 4 minutes but it's still adding roughly 3 hours of agent time a week. Ask whether moving the button back up is a quick change. Two paragraphs, factual, no blame, for the product manager who owns the page.","add one line on what support is telling customers in the meantime"]},{"match":"Build the onboarding deck for new hires.","surface":"cowork","full":"Build the onboarding deck for new hires. This is for the enablement team's first-day session, which runs for 90 minutes and covers who we are, how the business makes money, how teams fit together and what the first two weeks look like.\n\nWe have about 25 new joiners a month across sales, delivery and operations. The current deck is 48 slides and people glaze over by slide 15. I'd like no more than 20 slides, with speaker notes, and at least two moments where the room does something rather than listens.\n\nGive me an outline with slide titles, one line of content per slide and the speaker notes.","followUps":["Slide 3 history is too long. One slide, three milestones.","the 'how we make money' slide needs actual numbers. roughly 60% recurring subscriptions, 30% services, 10% training","We should split the last third by role. Sales, delivery and operations have very different first weeks: sales shadow calls from day two, delivery do two days of product training first, and operations spend the first week on systems access and process walkthroughs. Instead of one 'your first two weeks' slide, can you do three short role slides so the facilitator only shows the ones for people in the room? Adjust the speaker notes to say that.","Replace the quiz activity. The last cohort hated it. Something where they talk to each other instead.","The head of people reviewed the outline. She wants a slide on how we work: flexible hours with core hours of 10 to 3, two office days a week for most teams, and the expectation that cameras are on for internal meetings unless there's a reason not to. She also asked that we drop the org chart slide entirely because it's out of date within a month, and point people to the directory instead. Please make both changes and keep it at 20 slides."]},{"match":"Summarise these notes into a short update.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise these notes into a short update. They're my notes from the weekly ops sync, which I need to send to the wider team by end of day. Most readers weren't in the meeting, so assume no context.\n\nNotes:\n- warehouse move: lease signed, fit-out starts in 3 weeks, go live still targeted for end of Q1\n- picking accuracy 98.1%, up from 97.4\n- new carrier trial with Corvel Group going ok, 2 late collections last week, raising with them\n- hiring: 4 of 6 seasonal roles filled\n- need decision on weekend shifts by Friday\n\nAim for something people can read in a minute.","followUps":["the weekend shift decision should be first, it's the only thing that needs anyone to act","Drop the decimals. Just say picking accuracy is up again.","A couple of things I left out of the notes. The fit-out date has actually slipped a week because the electrical survey found the main board needs replacing, so it's 4 weeks not 3, but the go live date is still realistic because we'd built in a fortnight of slack. Say that clearly so nobody panics. Also, on Corvel: both late collections were on Mondays, and they've told us it's a driver rota problem they're fixing this week.","Good. Now a two sentence version for my manager, who only wants the decision and the risk.","One more version please, for the site noticeboard. Different audience: warehouse staff, many on shift, who'll read it on a printed sheet. They don't care about the carrier or the picking trend. They care about the move (what changes for them and when), the weekend shift question (we'll confirm by Friday and nobody will be rostered without two weeks' notice) and seasonal hiring (more hands from next month). Short sentences, no jargon."]},{"match":"Draft a first pass at this document.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft a first pass at this document. It's a one-page proposal to move our team's weekly status meeting from a 60 minute call to a written update plus a 20 minute call for decisions only. I'll take it to my director next week.\n\nThe argument: the call has grown to 14 people, most of whom speak for two minutes and listen for 58, and we've tried tightening the agenda twice without lasting change. Similar teams in the organisation have moved to written updates and stuck with them.\n\nStructure: what we do now, what I'm proposing, what we gain, what we risk, and how we'd trial it. Plain and persuasive, not salesy.","followUps":["you've made the risks sound trivial. my director will see through that. give them proper weight","Real numbers to add. 14 people for 60 minutes a week is 14 hours, call it 700 hours a year. If the decisions call is 20 minutes with 6 people, that's 2 hours a week, and writing an update takes each person about 15 minutes, so 3.5 hours. Net saving is roughly 8.5 hours a week. Can you put that in the gains section as a simple sum, and be clear it's an estimate based on attendance, not a measured figure?","For the trial section: six weeks, starting the first Monday of next month. Updates are due by Tuesday noon in the team channel, using a three heading template (done, next, needs a decision). The decisions call is Wednesday at 10 and only people with an item attend. We review at week 3 with a quick survey and again at week 6 before deciding whether to keep it. If fewer than 80% of updates arrive on time by week 3, we revisit the format. Please write that up properly.","What would make you call the trial a failure? Add two criteria.","Shorten the opening. Get to the proposal in the first two sentences."]},{"match":"Help me structure this into a clear report.","surface":"cowork","full":"Help me structure this into a clear report. I've got a mess of findings from a review of our supplier onboarding process and I need to turn it into something the procurement director can read in one sitting.\n\nWhat I have: interviews with 9 people across procurement, finance and legal; a sample of 30 recent onboardings, averaging 23 working days from request to first purchase order; a list of 11 pain points, some of which overlap; and four ideas for improvement that came up repeatedly.\n\nI don't want you to write the whole thing yet. Propose a structure with headings, what goes under each, and where the evidence sits.","followUps":["Put recommendations before findings. She reads the start and skips to the end.","Here's the breakdown of the 23 days from the sample, it might change the structure. Request to procurement approval: 4 days. Supplier forms returned by the supplier: 9 days on average, the biggest block and largely outside our control. Finance checks on supplier details: 3 days. Legal contract review: 5 days, but only for the 12 of 30 that needed a contract. Set-up in the system: 2 days. I think the report should be organised around these stages rather than the pain point list. What do you think, and can you redo the structure that way?","yes, go with stages. fold the 11 pain points into those","Now draft the executive summary, half a page, using that structure.","The summary oversells the supplier forms point. We can't make suppliers faster, but we can make the forms shorter and send them earlier, at the point the request is raised rather than after approval. Two of the four improvement ideas are exactly that. Rewrite that paragraph to focus on what we control, and add that the legal step only applies to 40% of onboardings, so the headline average hides the fact that most suppliers never wait for it."]},{"match":"Check the demo environment health before the customer call.","surface":"cowork","full":"Check the demo environment health before the customer call. We're showing the platform to Corvel Group's operations director at 2pm and the last demo had a dashboard that loaded empty, which was embarrassing.\n\nHere's what the health page and the admin panel show right now: service status healthy, last data sync 40 minutes ago, 3 scheduled jobs marked as failed overnight (weekly briefing, use case tracker, file summary), dashboard loads in about 4 seconds, demo dataset reseeded this morning.\n\nTell me what to worry about, what to ignore, and anything I should check by hand before 2pm.","followUps":["Is the weekly briefing failure going to show on screen? That's the card I open with.","Engineering looked at the failures. The briefing job failed because it ran before the reseed finished, so it had no data and refused to write. Rerunning it now should fix it. The use case tracker failure is a known timeout that doesn't affect anything visible. They're less sure about the file summary job. Given that, can you give me a short checklist of exactly what to click through at 1:30 to confirm the screens I'll show are populated, in the order I'll show them: dashboard, weekly briefing, the operations department view, and the spend page.","Briefing reran fine. Spend page shows last month only, is that expected on day 3?","ok. give me a fallback line if something does load empty while they're watching","Last thing. Their operations director will almost certainly ask how fresh the data is in a real deployment compared with the demo. I want an honest answer: in the demo it's seeded daily, in production the activity data arrives within a few minutes and the analytics refresh on different cycles, some every few minutes, some hourly, the briefings weekly. Draft two or three sentences I can say that don't overpromise and don't sound evasive."]},{"match":"Draft the exec briefing for this week's pipeline review.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the exec briefing for this week's pipeline review. Audience is the CEO, the CFO and the two regional sales leads. They get it Wednesday evening and the meeting is Thursday at 9.\n\nTop lines from the CRM report: quarter target £4.2m, closed so far £2.6m, weighted pipeline for the rest of the quarter £1.9m, but £1.1m of that sits in three deals. The northern region is ahead at 71% of target, the southern region is at 52%. Average sales cycle has stretched from 68 to 81 days since last quarter.\n\nOne page. Lead with whether we hit the number and what it depends on.","followUps":["Name the three big deals by client: Halden Logistics, Brightwater Retail, Corvel Group. Stage and expected close for each.","Updates from the deal owners this afternoon. Halden is at contract stage, legal have their redlines and we expect signature in two weeks, high confidence. Brightwater has slipped: their procurement lead has asked for a revised commercial proposal and won't commit before next quarter, so realistically that's out. Corvel is at proposal stage and has asked for a reference call with an existing customer. Please reflect those and recompute the outlook. Without Brightwater (£420k) we're relying on Halden plus the long tail.","CFO will want a range, not a single number. Give low, likely and high, with what each assumes.","On cycle length: the sales ops lead thinks the stretch from 68 to 81 days is mostly in the late stages, with deals sitting at proposal and contract for longer, because more customers now require a security review before signing. Of last quarter's closed deals, 9 of 22 had one, against 3 of 19 the quarter before. Give it its own short paragraph with that explanation, and suggest one thing we could do about it, such as having a standard security pack ready earlier in the cycle.","Too many adjectives. Tone it down, this group prefers flat statements."]},{"match":"Review the latest risk register update across departments.","surface":"cowork","full":"Review the latest risk register update across departments. Each department head submitted their quarterly update last week and I need to go through it with the risk committee on Monday. There are 38 risks in total, 7 new this quarter and 4 closed.\n\nWhat I'd like from you: spot any risks whose rating changed without a reason given, anything that looks under-rated given what we know, duplicates across departments, and mitigations that are vague ('monitor closely' type actions). I've pasted the register below as a table.\n\nBe direct. The committee prefers a short list of real issues to a long list of nitpicks.","followUps":["You flagged the two supplier risks as duplicates. They're not quite, one is supplier failure and one is supplier price rises. Keep them separate.","Context on the operations risk you said looked under-rated. The single warehouse dependency is rated medium likelihood, medium impact, and I agree that's low. Since the register was written we've had a flood warning for that site twice this autumn, and the business continuity plan assumes we can relocate within 10 days, which has never been tested. Draft a short note to the operations head asking them to re-rate it and explaining why, without making it sound like an accusation.","group your findings by department so I can hand each head their part","Which three would you put in front of the board? Just the three, one line each.","Finance pushed back on the vague mitigation comment for the foreign exchange risk. Their actual plan is to hedge 50% of forecast euro receipts for the next two quarters, with a review at each quarter end. They just wrote 'monitor exposure' in the register for brevity. Please withdraw that finding and add a general recommendation that mitigations should state the action, the owner and the review point, so we stop getting one-line entries."]},{"match":"Summarise the partner enablement feedback from this month.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise the partner enablement feedback from this month. We ran four enablement sessions for reseller partners and sent a survey afterwards. I need a summary for the partnerships leadership meeting next Tuesday.\n\nSurvey: 57 responses from 23 partner firms. Average rating 3.8 out of 5 (last month 4.1). Free text comments pasted below, about 40 of them. The main complaints I noticed were that the technical sessions went too deep too fast and that the sales playbook was out of date on pricing.\n\nWhat I want: the overall picture, the top three themes with a representative comment each, and what we should change for next month.","followUps":["Don't quote partners by firm. Leadership will forward this.","The drop from 4.1 to 3.8 needs context. This month 9 firms attended for the first time, all smaller resellers, and their average was 3.3. Returning firms averaged 4.0, roughly the same as before. So the drop is mostly about new partners finding the sessions pitched too high, not existing partners getting less value. Please make that the headline and adjust the recommendations, perhaps with a separate foundation session for new partners.","The pricing point is fixed already, the playbook was updated on the 20th. Mention it as done.","Here are the comments from the new partners specifically, so the themes reflect them properly: 'I didn't know what half the acronyms meant', 'would have liked a demo before the architecture deep dive', 'good content but needed the slides in advance', 'the hands-on lab assumed we already had a trial set up'. Pull out what they have in common and turn it into two concrete changes for the foundation session, with who on our side would own each.","add a line on attendance. 4 sessions, 112 registered, 81 attended","Make it fit on one slide. Headline, three themes, two actions."]},{"match":"Pull together the security posture summary for the customer questionnaire.","surface":"cowork","full":"Pull together the security posture summary for the customer questionnaire. Brightwater Retail's procurement team sent a 90 question security questionnaire as part of the contract process and wants a two-page posture summary alongside it.\n\nWhat we can say with evidence: data encrypted in transit and at rest, single sign-on and multi-factor authentication for all staff, role-based access with quarterly access reviews, an annual penetration test by an external firm (the last one in June), an incident response plan tested twice a year, and hosting in a UK region.\n\nKeep claims to what we can evidence. No marketing language.","followUps":["We're aligned with ISO 27001, not certified. Fix the wording everywhere it says certified.","Their questionnaire has a whole section on AI, which our summary doesn't cover. What we can say: we use a third party model provider for some analytics features, customer content sent to it is not used for training, data is processed under our existing data processing agreement, and customers can switch the AI features off in the admin settings. Prompts are also scanned for personal data before anything is stored. Add a short section on this in the same tone as the rest.","What will they ask next based on this? List the 5 follow-up questions I should expect.","add a line on sub-processors. we keep a list and notify customers 30 days before adding one","Legal reviewed the draft and asked for two changes. First, on the penetration test, say 'all high findings from the most recent test have been remediated', because two were found and fixed, and the questionnaire asks about that history directly. Second, on data location, say primary hosting is in the UK and backups are held in a second UK region, because they'll ask about backups separately. Everything else is fine."]},{"match":"Review the latest use-case governance rollup before the board update.","surface":"cowork","full":"Review the latest use-case governance rollup before the board update. The rollup covers the 11 AI use cases we've marked as strategic bets. The board meets in ten days and I'll present a five minute section on it.\n\nHeadline from the page: 4 adopted, 5 piloting, 1 at risk, 1 not seen. Estimated hours recovered over the quarter about 1,900, against measured active time of about 640 hours on those use cases. The at-risk one is contract summarisation in legal, which was strong in the summer and has gone quiet for three weeks.\n\nWhat would a sceptical board member pick at, and what should I check before I put my name to these numbers?","followUps":["The 1,900 is an estimate from a 30 minutes per session assumption. How do I present that without it sounding made up?","I asked the legal team about contract summarisation. It's gone quiet because the two people driving it moved onto a large due diligence project and are using a different workflow for that, which our matching rule doesn't pick up because it uses different terms. So it's not abandonment, it's a rule that's too narrow. Can you suggest how I describe that honestly to the board, and what I'd change in the matching rule?","The not seen one is the procurement assistant. Registered in August, nobody has used it. Retire it or explain it?","Draft my five minutes as speaker notes. Lead with outcomes, not counts.","The board member who chairs audit always asks how we know the numbers are right. Answer to prepare: session counts and active time come straight from usage telemetry, hours recovered are an estimate from an assumption we set, and the lifecycle labels follow fixed rules (adopted means active in three of the last four weeks with at least two people). Put that in a short appendix slide in plain English, and make the measured versus estimated split obvious."]},{"match":"Draft the exec summary for this quarter's FinOps review.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the exec summary for this quarter's FinOps review. The audience is the CFO and the leadership team, who want to know what we spent on AI tools, whether it's in line with plan, and where the money is going.\n\nFigures: total AI spend for the quarter £186k against a plan of £170k. By product: coding assistant £104k, collaborative workspace £61k, chat £21k. Engineering accounts for 58% of spend. Spend per active user went from £41 to £48 a month. About £9k is attributed to model usage we price at list rate, so it's an estimate.\n\nHalf a page. Lead with the variance and its cause.","followUps":["Is the £16k overspend a problem or expected? Plan assumed 140 users, we had 162.","Some detail on the coding assistant figure. Most of the increase came from a handful of long-running sessions on the most expensive model, scheduled tasks that a couple of teams set up in August and left running daily. Moving those to a mid-tier model would cut their cost by roughly 60% with no obvious loss of quality on the tasks they do. Add a short paragraph on that as the main lever for next quarter, and be clear the saving is an estimate.","Remove the per-department table. Leadership doesn't need it in the summary, put it in an appendix.","the £9k list rate caveat should be a footnote, not a paragraph","The CFO will ask about value, not just cost. We have two numbers we can stand behind: AI-assisted pull requests merged went from 410 to 590 over the quarter, and the share of workspace sessions that produced a file or document was 41%. Neither is a return on investment and I don't want it to read like one. Add a short 'what we got for it' section that states those plainly and says what we can't yet measure."]},{"match":"Check the classifier rubric change for unintended drift before it ships.","surface":"cowork","full":"Check the classifier rubric change for unintended drift before it ships. The analytics team has edited the definitions of the three delegation tiers that classify how much work people hand to Claude. The main change is that tier 3 now requires the task to span multiple steps with minimal correction, where before it only needed a substantial output.\n\nI've pasted the old and new rubric text below, plus a sample of 40 recent prompts with the tier each was given under the old rubric. Can you reclassify the sample under the new rubric, show me which ones move, and tell me whether the moves are what the change intended or side effects?","followUps":["8 moved from tier 3 to tier 2. Are any of those wrong under the new wording?","Two of your reclassifications look off. The quarterly forecast prompt you moved to tier 2 did span multiple steps: the person asked Claude to pull three data sources together, reconcile them and then draft commentary, with only one small correction. That should stay tier 3. And the meeting notes one you kept at tier 3 was a single-step summary of a long transcript, which is exactly what the change was meant to move down. Can you recheck the sample with those two as reference cases?","What does this do to the headline trend? If tier 3 share drops 10 points overnight, leadership will read it as people regressing.","Agreed on that risk. The team's plan is to mark the rubric change date on the charts and only compare like for like within each rubric version. I'm not sure that's enough for the monthly exec pack, which shows a single line. Draft a two sentence note to go under that chart for the next three months, explaining that the drop reflects a definition change and not a change in how people work.","Last thing: any wording in the new rubric that's ambiguous enough to cause inconsistent results? Quote it and suggest a fix."]},{"match":"Summarise the top risks flagged across departments this week.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise the top risks flagged across departments this week. I chair the Friday leadership call and want a short risk section at the top of the agenda.\n\nFrom the department updates: finance flagged that two large customer invoices are over 60 days overdue (£210k combined); operations flagged a key supplier giving notice on a contract that ends in March; HR flagged that attrition in customer support hit 9% this quarter; sales flagged the slipping Brightwater deal; IT flagged that the old reporting server goes out of vendor support in eight weeks.\n\nRank them by what needs leadership attention this week, not by size.","followUps":["the supplier one is higher than you've put it. there's no second source","More context on the reporting server. Migration to the replacement is planned and about 70% done, but the last 30% includes the finance month-end reports, and the finance team can't spare anyone to test until after the quarter close. So the realistic finish is two weeks after support ends. That's the actual risk: a short gap where the server is unsupported during a month-end. Please reword that item and suggest what we'd ask of finance.","HR attrition: 9% is quarterly, so roughly a third annualised. Say it that way, it lands better.","Write it as five lines, one per risk: what it is, why it matters this week, and who owns the next step.","On the overdue invoices, finance have now told me one of the two (£130k) is with Halden Logistics and is held up by a purchase order mismatch on their side, which their accounts team has acknowledged. The other (£80k) is with a smaller client who has gone quiet. They're different risks: one is admin, one may be a credit problem. Split them, and move the Halden one down the list."]},{"match":"Turn these customer call notes into a follow-up brief.","surface":"cowork","full":"Turn these customer call notes into a follow-up brief. I had a 45 minute call with Halden Logistics' head of operations and their procurement lead about expanding from one depot to all six. My notes are rough, pasted below.\n\nnotes: happy with the pilot depot, picking errors down noticeably. worried about rollout disruption in peak (Nov to Jan). procurement wants per-depot pricing not a flat fee. asked about integration with their warehouse system, said they'd send the spec. want a reference from someone at similar scale. decision by end of month.\n\nThe brief is for our account team: what they asked for, what we owe them, by when, and who on our side does it.","followUps":["Add a section on what we think their real concern is. I read the peak point as the deciding factor.","I remembered a few more things from the call. Their head of operations mentioned that their previous system rollout three years ago went badly and they lost two weeks of productivity, which is why peak worries them so much. Procurement also asked whether we'd accept a staged contract, two depots first and the rest in spring, with pricing locked for the whole term. Add both, and suggest how we'd respond on the staged option.","Owners: pricing goes to the commercial lead, integration to the solutions architect, the reference to me.","deadline for us is the 20th, gives them 10 days before their decision","Now draft the follow-up message to their head of operations, to go out tomorrow. Thank them, confirm the three things we'll send (revised pricing including a staged option, an integration approach once we have their spec, and a reference call with a customer of similar size), and propose a conversation about the rollout plan before their decision. Keep it to five or six sentences, warm but businesslike, no hard sell."]},{"match":"Draft a follow-up email after today's discovery call.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft a follow-up email after today's discovery call.\n\nThe prospect is Halden Logistics, a mid-sized freight business with about 400 staff across three depots. On the call were their operations director and the head of IT. The main pains they raised: route planning still lives in spreadsheets, drivers' paperwork comes back days late, and finance can't reconcile fuel costs until month end.\n\nKeep it warm but brief, no more than 200 words. Thank them, play back the three pains in their words, propose a 45-minute technical deep dive next week and say I'll send a short case study from a similar freight client. No hard sell, they were clear they're only at the research stage.","followUps":["Good. Drop the case study line, I've just checked and we can't share that one externally yet.","One correction: the head of IT wasn't actually sold on anything, she was the sceptical one. Her concern was integration with their existing warehouse system and whether we'd need access to their on-premises database. Can you add a sentence acknowledging that and saying we'll bring our solutions engineer to the deep dive specifically to walk through the integration options? Also the operations director said they're budgeting for this in Q2, not Q1, so don't imply urgency anywhere. Keep the tone the same.","Can you give me two options for the subject line, one plain and one a bit warmer?","Make the meeting ask more specific: offer Tuesday or Thursday afternoon.","Last thing. They asked for something they can forward internally to the finance director, who wasn't on the call. Can you write a separate 4 to 5 sentence paragraph I can paste underneath as a 'for anyone who wasn't on the call' summary? It should explain the problem in finance terms (late fuel reconciliation, manual effort in the month-end close) rather than operations terms, and avoid any product jargon. Assume the finance director has never heard of us."]},{"match":"Build a mutual action plan for the enterprise opportunity.","surface":"cowork","full":"Build a mutual action plan for the enterprise opportunity.\n\nThe opportunity is Corvel Group, around £310k a year over three years, and our champion is their director of operations. We're aiming for signature this quarter. Known steps on their side: business case sign-off, IT security review, legal review of our MSA, procurement, and a pilot with one region before full rollout.\n\nI want a plan we can share with the champion, so keep it neutral and practical, with owners on both sides. Our side has me as account lead, a solutions engineer and our legal team. Flag anything that usually takes longer than people expect.","followUps":["Put it in a table: step, owner (their side or ours), target week, status.","Just got off a call with our champion and the picture has changed. Their security review takes 6 weeks minimum, not 3, and it can't start until we've completed their vendor questionnaire (about 180 questions). Procurement needs three quotes even for a preferred supplier, so expect a formal RFP step. Their CFO signs anything over £250k and only at the monthly investment committee, which meets on the last Thursday of each month. Rework the plan backwards from the committee date at the end of next quarter rather than this one.","Add a column for 'risk if this slips' with one line each.","the pilot step is too vague. split it into scoping, setup, two-week run, readout.","Now write a short covering note to send to the champion with the plan attached. Frame it as a draft for us to agree together, not our plan imposed on them. Ask them to check the owners on their side because I've guessed at least two of them (I put their IT security lead against the questionnaire and their procurement manager against the RFP, but it might be the commercial team). Keep it under 150 words and finish with a suggestion to walk through it on a 20-minute call.","Shorter version of the table for the forecast call, top 5 milestones only."]},{"match":"Summarise this call transcript into next steps and objections.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise this call transcript into next steps and objections.\n\nIt's a 50-minute call with Brightwater Retail. I need it for the CRM notes and to brief my manager before Friday's pipeline review. Pasting the key part below, the rest was introductions.\n\nTheir CFO: we looked at something similar two years ago and the rollout stalled in the stores.\nTheir head of retail ops: our store managers won't use another app, they already have four.\nTheir CFO: if you can show payback inside twelve months I can take it to the board.\nMe: we could start with a pilot in five stores.\nTheir head of retail ops: five is fine but not before the January sales are over.","followUps":["Split the objections into 'real blockers' and 'concerns we can handle'.","You've listed price as an objection but nobody mentioned price. Remove it.","Here's the bit of the transcript I left out, it changes the next steps. 'Their CFO: I'd want our finance analyst to build the payback model with you, not just take your numbers. Their head of retail ops: and I'd want the area managers involved in choosing which five stores, otherwise they'll pick the easy ones. Their CFO: send us a proposal for the pilot by the end of the month and we'll look at it at our planning meeting.' Add the joint payback model and the store selection as next steps with owners, and put the proposal deadline at the top.","Now give me the 3-line version for the opportunity notes field in the CRM.","My manager will ask why I think this one is real when their last attempt stalled. Write me 4 or 5 bullet points I can use in the pipeline review that make the case honestly: what's different this time, what evidence we have from the call, and what would make me downgrade it. Don't oversell. If the evidence is thin on something, say so."]},{"match":"Prep talking points for tomorrow's renewal conversation.","surface":"cowork","full":"Prep talking points for tomorrow's renewal conversation.\n\nThe customer is Northgate Housing, renewal due in eight weeks at £86k a year. Our long-standing champion left in the summer and I'm meeting her replacement, the new head of digital, for the first time. It's a 30-minute video call.\n\nWhat I know: usage is down about 18% on last year, they've had a reorganisation, and the contract has a 4% price uplift built in. I'd like talking points that build the relationship first, cover the value they've had, and get a clear sense of whether she sees us as part of her plans. Bullet points, not a script.","followUps":["Lead with value delivered, not the usage dip.","Here's what the account dashboard shows for the last 12 months: active users went from 140 to 115, but the teams still using it are using it more (average sessions per user up 22%). The housing repairs team is the heaviest user; the tenant services team dropped off almost entirely after the reorganisation in spring. Support tickets were low, 9 in the year, all resolved. Rework the talking points so the story is 'concentrated value in repairs, a gap in tenant services we can help close' rather than a decline.","What questions should I ask her in the first ten minutes?","She may ask for a discount. Give me two fallback positions that don't involve cutting the price.","Thinking about it more, I don't want to go in with a big expansion pitch. She's new and will want to understand what she's inherited first. Can you restructure the whole thing into three parts: (1) listen and understand her priorities, (2) a short recap of what the account has achieved, (3) a proposal for a joint review in 60 days before we talk about renewal terms in detail. Keep it to one page I can glance at during the call."]},{"match":"Draft the launch announcement for the new feature.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the launch announcement for the new feature.\n\nThe feature is scheduled reports: users can set any dashboard to be emailed to colleagues on a regular schedule instead of exporting it and sending it by hand. It goes live for customers at the start of next month. The announcement will go on our blog and be linked from an in-app message, so the readers are existing customers, mostly operations and finance managers.\n\nAbout 400 words. Lead with the problem it solves, explain how it works in two or three sentences, and end with where to find it. Our house style is plain and practical, not hype.","followUps":["Too salesy. Tone it down and cut the exclamation marks.","The product team sent their notes, please work these in: reports can be scheduled daily, weekly or monthly; recipients don't need an account to view them; admins can restrict which dashboards can be scheduled; it's available on the Professional and Enterprise plans only, not Starter. They also asked us not to say 'real-time' anywhere because the data refreshes every hour, and not to promise PDF export yet, that's next quarter. Please check the current draft for both of those.","Write the 40-word in-app banner version.","Add a short 'how to get started' section with three steps.","Legal reviewed it and want two changes. We can't say it 'saves hours every week' without evidence, so either drop that or attribute it to the beta (in the beta, 12 customer teams said it replaced a manual weekly export). And the Starter plan line needs to be clearer so nobody on Starter thinks they're getting it. Can you make those edits and then give me the final version with a short post for our company social page underneath?"]},{"match":"Analyse last quarter's campaign performance by channel.","surface":"cowork","full":"Analyse last quarter's campaign performance by channel.\n\nThis is for the quarterly marketing review. Total spend was £171k. Figures from the CRM and the ad platforms:\n\nPaid search: £48k spend, 620 leads, 74 SQLs, £410k pipeline\nPaid social: £35k spend, 910 leads, 41 SQLs, £180k pipeline\nEvents: £60k spend, 210 leads, 58 SQLs, £520k pipeline\nEmail: £6k spend, 340 leads, 39 SQLs, £230k pipeline\nContent syndication: £22k spend, 480 leads, 12 SQLs, £45k pipeline\n\nI want to know which channels are actually producing pipeline rather than just leads, and where the conversion from lead to SQL breaks down.","followUps":["Add cost per SQL and pipeline per pound spent as columns.","Which channel would you cut first and why? One paragraph.","One thing missing: the events pipeline is inflated because two of the deals came from a single dinner our CEO hosted for existing customers, so arguably that's expansion rather than new business. Those two deals account for £210k of the £520k. Can you show events both with and without them? Also paid social had a creative refresh halfway through the quarter, and the first-half cost per lead was nearly double the second half, so I'd rather not judge it on the quarter average. Note that as a caveat.","keep the tone neutral. the paid social owner will read this.","Turn this into 5 slides' worth of content for the marketing leadership meeting: headline finding, channel table, the events caveat, the paid social story, and a recommendation for next quarter's budget split. For the recommendation assume the total budget stays at £171k. Give each slide a title and no more than 4 bullets."]},{"match":"Build the messaging brief for the new product line.","surface":"cowork","full":"Build the messaging brief for the new product line.\n\nWe're launching a smaller version of our workforce scheduling software aimed at care homes and small clinics, typically 20 to 80 staff. Our existing product is sold to hospital trusts and large groups, and it's too complex and too expensive for this market. The new line has rota planning, shift swaps on mobile and staffing ratio alerts, and is priced per site rather than per user.\n\nThe brief should cover: positioning statement, target audiences, the main problem we solve, three key messages with proof points, tone of voice, and things to avoid saying. It will be used by our own team and by an external copywriting agency.","followUps":["The positioning statement is too generic. Make it specific to care homes.","Here are notes from five customer interviews last month. Use them for the proof points and the pain language: 'rotas take my deputy two days a month', 'we get fined if ratios drop on a night shift', 'agency staff costs are killing us', 'I need to see on my phone who's in tomorrow', 'the big systems are built for hospitals, not 40-bed homes'. Keep their words where you can. Also, the sales team wants compliance to be the lead message, but I think it's cost. Give me your view based on these notes.","Add a section listing words to avoid, e.g. 'enterprise', 'synergy', 'workforce optimisation'.","Do a one-line elevator pitch for each of three audiences: owner, home manager, finance.","Final step: the brief is going to the agency writing the website copy and they've never worked in care. Add a short 'context for writers' section at the top explaining how a care home runs a rota, what a staffing ratio is in plain terms, and why agency staff are expensive. Keep it factual, no statistics we haven't sourced. Then give me the whole brief in one go so I can paste it into a document."]},{"match":"Suggest three subject lines for the launch newsletter.","surface":"cowork","full":"Suggest three subject lines for the launch newsletter.\n\nThe newsletter goes to about 12,000 subscribers next Tuesday and announces our new mobile app for expense approvals. Until now managers had to log in on a laptop to approve claims; with the app they can approve from their phone in a couple of taps. Our usual open rate is around 31%.\n\nNo capital letters for emphasis, no emoji, nothing that sounds like a sales promotion. Ideally under 50 characters so it doesn't get cut off on mobile.","followUps":["Give me three more that are shorter, under 35 characters.","Number 2 reads a bit like spam. Replace it with something plainer.","Context I should have given: the last two newsletters with question-style subject lines did noticeably worse (around 24% open rate against our usual 31%), and the ones naming a specific benefit did best. Our readers are mostly finance managers and office managers in small firms, not early adopters. So please avoid questions and lead with the benefit, which is that approvals can now be done in under a minute from a phone. Give me five options on that basis.","Write matching preview text for the top two.","We're going to A/B test two of these on 20% of the list before sending to the rest. Pick the two that are most different from each other in approach, explain in a sentence each what we'd learn from the test, and suggest how long to wait before picking a winner, given the list is around 12,000 people and most opens happen in the first day."]},{"match":"Review this vendor MSA for liability caps and auto-renewal.","surface":"cowork","full":"Review this vendor MSA for liability caps and auto-renewal.\n\nThe vendor is Tessaly Cloud Services, for document storage and sharing across the firm. Annual fees are about £140k. The two clauses I'm most worried about:\n\n14.1 Each party's total aggregate liability arising under or in connection with this Agreement shall not exceed the fees paid in the three (3) months preceding the event giving rise to the claim.\n\n18.2 This Agreement shall automatically renew for successive periods of twenty-four (24) months unless either party gives written notice not less than ninety (90) days before the end of the then-current term.\n\nOur standard position is a cap of 12 months' fees and renewal for 12 months with 60 days' notice. Tell me how far off they are and what to push for.","followUps":["Present it as a table: clause, their position, our standard, proposed redline.","Found the data protection clause too, please assess it alongside the cap: '14.3 Notwithstanding clause 14.1, the Supplier's liability for breach of clause 11 (Data Protection) shall not exceed one hundred thousand pounds (£100,000) in aggregate.' Our contract value is about £140k a year and the system will hold client files. Our playbook says data protection liability should be uncapped or subject to a super-cap of at least 3 times annual fees. Is £100k defensible as a fallback, or should we hold firm?","What's a reasonable fallback if they refuse to move the general cap at all?","redline wording for 18.2 please, ready to paste.","The business owner just wants to know if they can sign. Write a short note to the head of operations (who owns this vendor relationship) in plain English: the two issues, what we're asking for, what happens if the vendor says no, and whether it's sign or don't sign yet. Keep it under 250 words, no clause numbers in the main text, put them in brackets at the end if needed."]},{"match":"Summarise the redlines in the customer DPA.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise the redlines in the customer DPA.\n\nPellmont Insurance has sent back our standard data processing agreement with 23 tracked changes. The commercial lead on the deal needs to know which ones matter before a call with their procurement team on Thursday. Most of the changes look cosmetic, but a few touch breach notification, audit rights, sub-processors and deletion at the end of the contract.\n\nPlease summarise each substantive change in a sentence, say whether it shifts risk to us, and skip the purely editorial ones (defined terms, numbering, typos) apart from a count at the end.","followUps":["Group them as accept, negotiate, reject.","Here are the three I'm least sure about, please give me more detail on each: (1) they've changed our breach notification from 'without undue delay' to 'within 24 hours of becoming aware'; (2) they want on-site audit rights once a year at our cost rather than theirs; (3) they've added that we must get their prior written consent, not just notify them, before adding any new sub-processor. For each, what's the market norm, and what's the practical impact on our operations team?","Our ops team says 48 hours is the fastest they can commit to. Draft a counter on (1).","Shorter. The commercial lead only needs a paragraph and the reject list.","Draft the cover email back to their legal counsel. Accept the ones we agreed to, explain briefly our position on the sub-processor point (we'll offer 30 days' advance notice and a right to object instead of prior consent), propose 48 hours on breach notification, and suggest a call to close the audit point. Professional and collaborative, they're a new customer and we want this signed this month."]},{"match":"Check this contract clause for data-transfer compliance.","surface":"cowork","full":"Check this contract clause for data-transfer compliance.\n\nIt's from the agreement with our new payroll provider, Linbrook Payroll Services. Part of their support team is based outside the UK. The clause reads:\n\n'The Supplier may process Customer Personal Data in any country in which the Supplier or its affiliates operate, provided that the Supplier takes reasonable steps to protect such data.'\n\nThe data involved is employee payroll information for about 450 staff. I need to know whether this is adequate under the UK GDPR transfer rules and, if not, what the clause should say instead. We're aiming to sign within two weeks.","followUps":["What specifically is missing compared with what we'd normally expect?","The supplier came back with an update: their support team is in a country without an adequacy decision, and they rely on the international data transfer agreement plus a transfer risk assessment they carried out last year. They've offered to attach both as a schedule. They also say the support staff only see employee names and the text of payroll queries, never salary or bank information. Does that change your view, and what should we ask to see before we accept?","Draft replacement clause wording we could propose.","Keep it UK-focused, we don't need the EU angle on this one.","The data protection officer wants a short note for the record explaining the decision. Write a half-page file note: the clause as originally drafted, the issue, what the supplier provided, what we asked for, and the conclusion (accept subject to the revised clause and receiving a copy of the risk assessment). Neutral tone, past tense, no recommendations section."]},{"match":"Draft the standard NDA cover note.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the standard NDA cover note.\n\nThis is the short note sales send along with our mutual NDA when a prospect asks for one. At the moment they send the NDA with no explanation, and we get a lot of marked-up versions back or the prospect's own template, which slows everything down and lands on the legal team.\n\nThe note should explain that this is our standard mutual NDA, that it's balanced and widely accepted, and ask them to sign it as it stands where possible. It needs to work for small prospects and large corporates alike. Keep it short enough that someone will actually read it.","followUps":["Make it friendlier, it reads like a warning.","Sales leadership asked for a few practical points to be included. If the prospect insists on their own NDA, sales should forward it to the legal team's shared inbox and we'll aim to review it within 3 working days. The term (2 years) and governing law (England and Wales) are the two things we'll almost never change. And a one-way NDA is fine when we're only receiving information. Can you work those in without making the note longer than about 200 words?","Can you add a two-line version for when they reply by message rather than email?","Swap 'counterparty' for 'the other company' throughout.","Now write a short internal guide for the sales team to go with it, separate from the cover note. Something like 'when to send the NDA, when you don't need one, and what to do if the other side pushes back'. Five or six bullets at most. Assume the reader is a new account executive who's never dealt with the legal team before."]},{"match":"Reconcile the monthly P&L against budget and flag variances.","surface":"cowork","full":"Reconcile the monthly P&L against budget and flag variances.\n\nSeptember close. Figures from the accounting system against the approved budget:\n\nRevenue: actual £1.84m, budget £1.95m\nCost of sales: actual £0.74m, budget £0.78m\nStaff costs: actual £0.81m, budget £0.79m\nMarketing: actual £96k, budget £120k\nSoftware and licences: actual £142k, budget £70k\nTravel: actual £14k, budget £22k\n\nFlag anything more than 5% or £20k away from budget, whichever is smaller. Show the variance in pounds and as a percentage, and say whether each one is favourable or adverse.","followUps":["Adding year-to-date so you can show both. YTD actual against budget: revenue £16.2m vs £16.9m, cost of sales £6.6m vs £6.8m, staff costs £7.1m vs £6.95m, marketing £1.22m vs £1.35m, software and licences £0.71m vs £0.62m, travel £0.19m vs £0.24m. Please add YTD columns next to the month and apply the same flag rule. I want to see which of the monthly variances are one-offs and which are part of a trend.","Software: I think that's annual licences billed in one month. Flag it as timing, not overspend.","You've got the sign convention backwards on costs. Favourable should be positive.","Write up the flagged items as short bullet commentary for the CFO: one bullet per variance, what drove it, whether it's timing or permanent, and the likely full-year impact. Where we don't know the driver yet, say 'driver to be confirmed with the budget holder' rather than guessing. Order by size of variance, biggest first.","add a one-line headline at the top."]},{"match":"Build the revenue summary for the board pack.","surface":"cowork","full":"Build the revenue summary for the board pack.\n\nIt's the Q3 board pack and the revenue section needs to fit on one page. The board is mostly non-executive directors, so plain language and no internal shorthand.\n\nKey figures: recurring revenue £4.6m for the quarter, up 12% on the same quarter last year; services revenue £1.1m, down 8%; net revenue retention 108%; we lost three customers, together worth about £150k a year; the largest new contract was a three-year deal with Ostler Foods worth £420k in total. Please write a short headline, then sections on recurring revenue, services, retention, and notable wins and losses.","followUps":["Lead with the recurring revenue story, services is secondary.","The CFO wants a chart description included, since the board pack designer will build it. Quarterly recurring revenue for the last six quarters was £3.7m, £3.9m, £4.0m, £4.2m, £4.4m and £4.6m. Services over the same quarters was £1.3m, £1.2m, £1.25m, £1.2m, £1.15m and £1.1m. Describe a simple chart that shows recurring revenue growing and services gently declining, and write a one-sentence caption that doesn't overclaim.","Explain net revenue retention in one plain sentence for the non-execs.","Remove the bit about pipeline, that's covered in the sales section.","One of the non-execs always asks about customer concentration. Our top 10 customers are 41% of recurring revenue, down from 46% a year ago, and the largest single customer is 7%. Add a short concentration paragraph after the retention section. Then check the whole page is under 400 words and give me the final version."]},{"match":"Draft the variance commentary for management accounts.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the variance commentary for management accounts.\n\nSeptember management accounts go to the leadership team on Monday. The main variances against budget:\n\nRevenue: £62k below budget, two client projects went live later than planned\nContractor costs: £38k over budget, covering two vacant roles\nRent and rates: £15k favourable, business rates rebate received\nMarketing: £24k underspend, the autumn event moved to November\n\nEverything else is within tolerance. The commentary should explain each variance in plain terms and be honest about what's within our control and what isn't.","followUps":["Too long. One sentence per variance.","The rates rebate is a one-off, make sure that's clear.","Additional context from the budget holders. The two delayed projects went live this week, so the revenue should catch up in October and November, roughly £40k of the £62k. The contractor cost will continue until the two hires start in mid-November, after which it falls away. The event moved to November isn't a saving, it's the same £24k, just later. Rework the commentary so each line says whether it reverses, continues or is permanent.","Add a line at the bottom with the net effect on the full-year forecast.","The CEO prefers commentary written as a short narrative rather than bullets. Can you turn it into two paragraphs: the first on what happened this month and why, the second on what it means for the rest of the year. Plain language, no accounting abbreviations, and please don't use the word 'headwinds'."]},{"match":"Summarise vendor spend by category this quarter.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise vendor spend by category this quarter.\n\nThis is from the accounts payable export for Q3, around 340 invoices, already tagged by category. Totals:\n\nSoftware: £412k\nProfessional services: £286k\nFacilities: £154k\nMarketing agencies: £131k\nRecruitment: £97k\nTravel: £58k\nOther: £44k\n\nIt's for the quarterly procurement review with the head of procurement and the finance director. I'd like a short summary of where the money goes, the share of the total for each category, and anything that stands out.","followUps":["Q2 for comparison: software £365k, professional services £214k, facilities £150k, marketing £148k, recruitment £61k, travel £49k, other £39k.","Which three categories should procurement look at first?","Drilling into professional services: the increase is mostly two suppliers. Fenwick Advisory went from £40k to £96k (they picked up the systems migration project), and a new law firm, Ashby Partners, billed £38k for the office lease renegotiation. Recruitment is up because we used agencies for four senior hires instead of the internal team. Can you add a 'what's behind the change' line for each category that moved more than 10%, and mark which ones look one-off and which look recurring?","Don't call the recruitment spend 'excessive'. Neutral language please.","Procurement wants a short list of actions from this. Suggest up to five practical actions, for example consolidating overlapping software subscriptions or agreeing rate cards with our most frequent suppliers, each with the category it applies to and a rough idea of the saving we might target. Make it clear the savings are rough estimates for discussion, not commitments."]},{"match":"Summarise this week's top support tickets by theme.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise this week's top support tickets by theme.\n\nWe had 214 tickets this week, up from about 170 last week. I need a summary for Monday's product sync. A sample of the subject lines:\n\nExport to spreadsheet keeps timing out\nInvoice shows the wrong VAT amount\nCan't sign in after turning on two-step verification\nReport filters reset when I change page\nHow do I add a new user to my team\nExport stuck at 90%\n\nGroup them into themes, say roughly how big each theme is, and highlight anything that looks like a bug rather than a how-to question.","followUps":["Add a count and share of total for each theme.","Sign-in problems and account lockouts are really one theme, merge them.","Engineering has asked for more detail on the export failures. Here's what the notes on the 18 tickets in that theme say: most are customers exporting reports with more than 50,000 rows, where the export spins and times out after a few minutes; three are from the same customer, Maple and Rowe; two mention it worked fine last month; one says it only fails in the evening. Can you write a short bug-style summary for engineering: symptoms, pattern, what we know, and what we'd like them to check?","Which theme would you raise first at the product sync and why?","Turn the whole thing into a short update I can paste into the team channel: the three top themes with counts, one sentence on what's changed since last week (export failures up from 6 to 18, billing questions down after the invoice redesign), and one ask of the product team. Keep it under 120 words and friendly."]},{"match":"Draft a response to the escalated billing complaint.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft a response to the escalated billing complaint.\n\nThe customer is Ferncroft Dental Group, a group of six practices. In August they changed plans and were charged £1,240 twice. Their practice director has now escalated it, says nobody got back to them for a week, and is threatening to cancel. They also lost access to the system for most of a day because the account was wrongly marked as overdue.\n\nI want a reply that apologises properly, explains what happened without excuses, confirms what we're doing to put it right and makes it easy for them to talk to a person. Firm on facts, warm in tone.","followUps":["Don't blame the payment provider. We own this.","I've checked with billing and have more detail. The refund for the duplicate charge was issued yesterday and should land in 3 to 5 working days. The loss of access happened because our system flagged the account as overdue when the plan change failed halfway, which is our bug. Our CS director has approved a credit of one month's subscription (£1,240) on top of the refund. Please include all of that clearly, and add a line on what we've changed so it doesn't happen again: engineering has fixed the plan-change process and added a check before any account is suspended.","Offer a call with me this week rather than asking them to reply.","Too formal. They're a small business, write it like a person.","Draft a short internal note for the account record and our finance team: what happened, the customer impact, what we've agreed (refund plus one month's credit), who approved it, and a follow-up date to check the refund has landed. Also note that the customer said nobody replied for a week, so add a line for our team lead on where the original ticket stalled, which I'll confirm before the note is filed. Factual and concise, bullet points are fine."]},{"match":"Build the quarterly health-score summary for the top accounts.","surface":"cowork","full":"Build the quarterly health-score summary for the top accounts.\n\nIt's for the VP of customer success and the CRO. Scores run from 0 to 100 and combine product usage, support history, survey sentiment and payment behaviour. This quarter against last for the top accounts:\n\nOstler Foods: 88 (was 85)\nKinsale Media: 54 (was 72)\nRowanmere Health: 69 (was 66)\nTarrant Engineering: 49 (was 55)\nWexley Schools Trust: 51 (was 67)\nDunmore Utilities: 81 (was 80)\n\nI'd like a one-page summary: the overall picture, the accounts that moved most, and where we need to act before renewal.","followUps":["Group them into healthy, watch and at risk.","Here's what the account managers told me about the two biggest drops. Kinsale Media: their main sponsor moved to a new role and the replacement hasn't logged in yet; usage is down 30%. Wexley Schools Trust: they had a bad support experience over a data import in July (two tickets, both took more than a week to resolve) and the sentiment score is reflecting that. Add a 'why it moved' line for each account that dropped by 10 points or more, and a suggested next action.","Add one sentence explaining how the score is built so the CRO doesn't question it.","Cut the healthy accounts to a single line, focus the page on at risk.","Turn the at-risk section into a one-page action plan for the CRO: for each at-risk account, the renewal month, the annual value, the main risk, the owner and the next step with a target date. Renewals and values: Kinsale Media renews in February at £120k; Wexley Schools Trust renews in April at £64k; Tarrant Engineering renews in January at £88k. Order by renewal, earliest first, and use 'account manager' as the owner where I haven't said otherwise."]},{"match":"Turn this call transcript into a renewal risk note.","surface":"cowork","full":"Turn this call transcript into a renewal risk note.\n\nThe call was with Rowanmere Health's operations lead. Their renewal is in ten weeks. Key parts of the transcript:\n\nTheir ops lead: honestly the team likes it, but the board is asking us to cut software spend by 15%.\nTheir ops lead: we've been shown another system that does appointment reminders in bulk, which yours doesn't.\nMe: what would need to be true for you to renew?\nTheir ops lead: show me we're using it across all the clinics, and give me something for the reminders problem.\n\nI need a risk note for our renewals tracker: summary, risk factors, what they need, and our next steps.","followUps":["Rate the risk high, medium or low and say why in one sentence.","you've treated the other system as a passing comment. it's the main risk, rewrite to reflect that.","Additional context not in the transcript: their contract is £72k a year, they've been a customer for three years, and they signed a two-year term last time. Usage is steady but only in two of their five clinics. Our product team has the feature they asked about (bulk appointment reminders) on the roadmap for next quarter, but it isn't committed. Please add a section with the commercial facts, and be careful not to promise the roadmap item.","Suggest a save plan in three steps.","Now write the internal escalation message to my manager and the account executive. It should explain the risk in a few sentences, what I'm planning to do, and what I need from them: the account executive to join a call with their finance lead, and my manager to ask product for a firmer date on the reminders feature. Keep it under 150 words and straightforward."]},{"match":"Draft the onboarding checklist for a new starter.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the onboarding checklist for a new starter.\n\nWe have a management accountant joining the finance team in three weeks. They'll be hybrid, in the office three days a week. Our current onboarding is a mix of emails and whatever the line manager remembers, and the last two starters both spent their first morning without a working laptop.\n\nI'd like a checklist for the line manager covering before day one, the first day, the first week and the first month. Practical items only, and make it reusable for other roles with small changes.","followUps":["Split it into what HR does and what the line manager does.","IT just told me a few things that need to be on the list: laptop requests need 10 working days' notice, the starter has to complete security awareness training before they're given access to the finance systems, and building passes are only issued on the first day if a photo has been sent to facilities in advance. Also our probation review is at 3 months, with a check-in at 6 weeks. Please add all of these in the right places.","Add a buddy section, we assign a buddy from another team.","make it a table i can paste into a doc: task, owner, when, done.","This is working well. Can you produce a shorter version for the new starter themselves, written to them directly: what to expect before they start, what their first day looks like, and who to ask about what (by role, not by name). Friendly tone, no more than a page, and avoid HR jargon like 'onboarding journey'."]},{"match":"Summarise the engagement-survey themes.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise the engagement-survey themes.\n\nWe had 640 responses, a 71% response rate. Headline scores out of 100, with last year in brackets: overall engagement 68 (72), manager support 78 (77), career growth 52 (58), workload 55 (60), recognition 61 (60). There are also about 380 free-text comments.\n\nA few typical comments: 'I don't know what the next step in my career here looks like', 'my manager is great but the team is stretched too thin', 'priorities change too often mid-quarter'. The summary is for the people director and the exec team. Be balanced: show what's improved as well as what's dropped.","followUps":["Pull out three themes from the comments with a representative quote for each.","Here are the department-level scores for career growth, which is the weakest area: Engineering 48, Sales 61, Customer Success 44, Finance 57, Operations 50, Marketing 59. Departments with fewer than 10 responses are excluded for anonymity, so Legal and HR aren't shown. Can you add a short section on where career growth is weakest, and make sure nothing in the summary could identify an individual or a small team?","Don't speculate on causes. Stick to what people actually said.","Shorter: 5 bullets for the exec team.","The exec team wants to share the results with all staff next week. Draft an all-staff message from the people director: thank people for taking part, share the headline results honestly including what went down, name the two areas we'll focus on (career growth and workload), and say we'll come back with actions within 6 weeks after talking to teams. Warm and honest, no corporate phrases like 'on a journey'."]},{"match":"Update the interview scorecard template.","surface":"cowork","full":"Update the interview scorecard template.\n\nThe current template asks interviewers to score candidates from 1 to 5 on 'culture fit', 'communication', 'technical ability' and 'attitude', with a free-text box at the end. In practice the scores vary wildly between interviewers and nobody can explain what a 3 actually means.\n\nI'd like a more structured version: competencies tied to the role, a defined scale, space for evidence, and a clear final recommendation. We'll pilot it on the customer success manager role first, then roll it out to other roles.","followUps":["Replace 'culture fit' with something we can actually assess.","Here are the competencies the hiring manager wants for the customer success manager role: handling difficult conversations, commercial awareness (spotting renewal risk and expansion opportunities), organising a portfolio of 30 to 40 accounts, and working with product to feed back customer issues. For each one, give me a behavioural interview question and descriptions of what a 1, a 3 and a 5 answer look like. Keep each description to one sentence.","Add a notes box and a final recommendation: hire, no hire, or discuss.","Can you add a short instruction at the top telling interviewers to score independently before the debrief?","Our talent lead raised a concern: interviewers sometimes write notes about things that aren't relevant or appropriate, like someone's accent or whether they 'seem young'. Add a short guidance section to the template on what to write in the notes (evidence against the competencies, specific examples the candidate gave) and what not to write. Keep it practical rather than legalistic, three or four lines."]},{"match":"Draft the FAQ for the new benefits rollout.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the FAQ for the new benefits rollout.\n\nFrom January we're introducing four changes: the employer pension match goes up from 5% to 6%, there's a new healthcare cash plan covering dental, optical and physiotherapy costs, a cycle-to-work scheme, and one extra paid wellbeing day a year. Enrolment for the cash plan and the cycle scheme runs for the first three weeks of December.\n\nThe FAQ goes to all staff on the intranet. Plain English, short answers, and assume most people won't read the original policy documents. Cover who's eligible, what it costs them and how to sign up.","followUps":["Group the questions under headings: pension, healthcare, other.","Some questions came in from the pilot group that need adding. Can part-time staff join the cash plan? (Yes, all employees on a permanent contract or a fixed-term contract of more than 6 months.) Is the cash plan taxable? (It's a taxable benefit and will appear on the year-end benefits form; payroll will explain.) Can I carry over the wellbeing day? (No, it must be used within the calendar year.) What happens to my pension match if I'm on parental leave? (The employer contribution continues on the same basis as now.) Please add these in the right sections.","The pension answer is confusing. Explain the match with a simple example on a £30k salary.","Add a 'who to contact' section, by role not name.","Final step: we also need a much shorter version for the intranet homepage banner and the all-staff meeting slide. Give me a 50-word summary of the four changes and the enrolment window, plus three bullet points for a single slide. Then double-check the full FAQ for anything that sounds like a promise we can't keep, for example anything implying the cash plan covers every treatment."]},{"match":"Add error handling to the webhook handler.","surface":"code","full":"Add error handling to the webhook handler. It's in src/webhooks/handler.ts and right now any exception inside processEvent bubbles up as a 500, so the sender retries the same delivery for hours and we end up with duplicate rows in the orders table.\n\nWhat I want: validate the payload shape up front and return 400 on a bad body, catch known downstream failures (the inventory service timing out) and return 503 so the retry is legitimate, and log everything else with the event id before returning 500. This is what the logs show at the moment:\n\nTypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'lineItems')\n    at processEvent (src/webhooks/handler.ts:58:31)\n    at async route (src/webhooks/router.ts:22:5)\n\nPlease keep the existing signature verification exactly as it is.","followUps":["use the zod schema we already have in src/webhooks/schemas.ts rather than writing a new validator","Two tests failing after your change:\n\nFAIL test/webhooks/handler.test.ts\n  x returns 503 when inventory times out\n    Expected: 503\n    Received: 500\n\nI think it's because the inventory client wraps the timeout in its own InventoryError and your instanceof check is looking for TimeoutError. Check src/clients/inventory.ts, the original error is on err.cause. Can you unwrap that properly rather than string matching on the message?","good. also make sure we never log the raw body, only the event id and type","Reviewer left this on the draft PR: \"Returning 400 for schema failures means the sender drops the event permanently. Are we sure every schema failure is the sender's fault? We added two optional fields last month and an older consumer version would have rejected them.\" Fair point. Can we make the schema pass through unknown keys, and only 400 when a field we actually read is missing or the wrong type? Add a test with an extra unknown field to prove it.","write the commit message, keep the subject under 60 chars"]},{"match":"Debug why the nightly job times out on large batches.","surface":"code","full":"Debug why the nightly job times out on large batches. The job is jobs/nightly-rollup.ts, it runs at 02:00 and has a 45 minute ceiling in the scheduler. On normal nights it finishes in about 12 minutes, but at month-end it processes roughly four times the rows and gets killed.\n\nTail of last night's log:\n\n[rollup] batch 37/112 committed (5000 rows) 31.2s\n[rollup] batch 38/112 committed (5000 rows) 44.8s\n[rollup] batch 39/112 committed (5000 rows) 58.1s\n[scheduler] job nightly-rollup exceeded 2700s, terminating\n\nEach batch gets slower, which makes me think something grows per batch rather than the data simply being big. I'd like the cause before any fix.","followUps":["ok that makes sense, the seen-ids Set is never cleared. but is that really enough to explain 58s per batch?","Ran it locally against the month-end snapshot with your logging in. Memory is flat after the Set change, but the batches still slow down:\n\nbatch 10 query 2.1s write 1.0s\nbatch 30 query 9.8s write 1.1s\nbatch 50 query 21.4s write 1.0s\n\nSo it's the read side. The query pages with OFFSET (see fetchBatch in jobs/rollup/source.ts). Is that the real culprit? If so, switch to keyset paging on (created_at, id) and keep the ordering identical.","there's already an index on (created_at, id), don't add another one","run it again against the snapshot and paste me the timings","Much better, 112 batches in just under 9 minutes. One thing worries me: rows inserted during the run with a created_at earlier than the cursor (late events from the queue) would now be skipped, whereas OFFSET would sometimes catch them by accident. Can you add a note to the job's docstring explaining this, and a sweep at the end that picks up anything with ingested_at after the job started? Keep it simple, one extra query.","commit this as two commits: the memory fix and the paging change"]},{"match":"Write unit tests for the rate-limiter middleware.","surface":"code","full":"Write unit tests for the rate-limiter middleware. It's src/middleware/rateLimit.ts, a sliding window keyed on client id, backed by Redis in production and an in-memory map in tests. There are no tests at all at the moment and we've had two regressions this quarter.\n\nCover at least: requests under the limit pass, the request that tips over the limit gets 429 with a Retry-After header, the window actually slides (not a fixed reset), and separate clients don't share a count. We use Jest with fake timers elsewhere, see test/middleware/cors.test.ts for the house style. Please don't change the middleware itself unless a test exposes a real bug.","followUps":["use jest.useFakeTimers({ now: 0 }) the same way the cors tests do, not Date.now mocking","One of the new tests fails and I think it's found a real bug:\n\n  x Retry-After reflects time until the oldest request leaves the window\n    Expected: \"12\"\n    Received: \"60\"\n\nThe middleware just sets Retry-After to the full window length. Before you fix it, check how the Redis store computes it in src/middleware/rateLimitStore.redis.ts, because if the two stores disagree I want them both corrected the same way.","fine, fix it in both stores. small change please","add one test for a client id with a colon in it, we had keys collide on that before","Coverage says rateLimit.ts is at 94% but the uncovered branch is the one where the store throws, which in production means Redis being down. Current behaviour is to fail open and let the request through, which is what we want, but it's untested. Add a test where the store rejects and assert the request passes and a warning is logged once, not on every request.","all green. open a PR against develop with a short description"]},{"match":"Refactor the auth module to use async/await throughout.","surface":"code","full":"Refactor the auth module to use async/await throughout. src/auth/ is a mix of three styles: old callback functions in session.js, promise chains in login.js, and a couple of newer async functions in mfa.ts. It makes the error handling hard to follow, and we've had one bug where a rejected promise was swallowed in a .then without a .catch.\n\nThe goal is behaviour-identical: same exports, same return values, same errors thrown. The callback-style functions are still called from two places in src/legacy/, so either update those callers or keep a thin callback wrapper, whichever is less risky. Existing tests are in test/auth/ and must keep passing without edits.","followUps":["update the legacy callers, I'd rather not keep the wrappers around","test/auth/session.test.ts is failing now:\n\n  x expires a session after idle timeout\n    Error: Timeout - Async callback was not invoked within the 5000 ms timeout\n\nThe test still uses the done callback and calls getSession(id, cb). You said tests wouldn't need edits but you changed getSession to return a promise. Either restore the callback signature for getSession or update this one test, but tell me which you picked and why before doing it.","ok update the test, that's fair","you've left a floating promise in logout(), the audit write isn't awaited","Reviewer comment on the PR: \"In login.js the old code fired the lastLoginAt update and returned without waiting for it, on purpose, so a slow write never held up the login response. Now it's awaited, which adds a round trip to every login.\" They're right that it was deliberate. Can you restore the fire-and-forget, but with an explicit .catch that logs, so we don't bring back the swallowed-rejection bug we started with?"]},{"match":"Optimise this slow Postgres query.","surface":"code","full":"Optimise this slow Postgres query. It backs the account activity page and takes 4 to 6 seconds on production-sized data:\n\nSELECT a.id, a.name, count(e.id) AS events, max(e.occurred_at) AS last_seen\nFROM accounts a\nLEFT JOIN events e ON e.account_id = a.id AND e.occurred_at > now() - interval '30 days'\nWHERE a.region = $1\nGROUP BY a.id, a.name\nORDER BY last_seen DESC NULLS LAST\nLIMIT 50;\n\nevents has about 40 million rows, accounts about 20 thousand. The query lives in src/db/queries/activity.ts. Ask if you need the plan.","followUps":["Here's EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) from the staging box:\n\nLimit (actual time=5121.4..5121.5 rows=50)\n  -> Sort (actual time=5121.4..5121.4 rows=50)\n     -> HashAggregate (actual time=5098.2..5110.7 rows=3914)\n        -> Hash Right Join (actual time=12.3..4380.9 rows=2871004)\n           -> Seq Scan on events e (actual time=0.02..3102.6 rows=6912330)\n                Filter: (occurred_at > (now() - '30 days'::interval))\n\nSo it's scanning the whole events table. There's only an index on events(account_id).","go with the composite index, but write it as a migration with CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY","migrations live in db/migrations, numbered. next free number is 0142","Applied the index on staging. Down to about 900ms, better but not great. The new plan shows a Nested Loop with an Index Only Scan per account, 3,914 loops. Would a lateral subquery with LIMIT 1 for last_seen help, or is the count the expensive part? Product only shows the count as a badge, so an approximate number above 999 is acceptable if that opens up a cheaper approach. Don't change the response shape though.","nice, 140ms. update the comment above the query to explain the lateral join"]},{"match":"Review this PR for race conditions.","surface":"code","full":"Review this PR for race conditions. It's the branch feature/seat-allocation, mainly src/billing/seats.ts and src/billing/seatsRepo.ts. It lets team admins assign seats from a fixed pool, and the check is: read the count of assigned seats, compare with the pool size, insert a new assignment if there's room.\n\nI'm worried two admins assigning at the same moment can both pass the check and overshoot the pool. There's also a background job that reclaims seats from deactivated users, which touches the same rows. Please read the diff against main and list anything that can interleave badly, with the sequence of steps that would cause it. Don't fix anything yet.","followUps":["the overshoot one is what I expected. what about the reclaim job vs a reassign of the same user?","Agree with your findings 1 and 3. On finding 2 I think you've misread it: reclaimSeats runs inside a transaction with SELECT ... FOR UPDATE on the seat rows (line 88 of seatsRepo.ts), so a concurrent assign for the same user would block on that lock. Can you re-check that one against the actual code and either withdraw it or show me the interleaving that still gets through the lock?","ok, withdrawn makes sense. now propose a fix for 1, I'm leaning towards a row lock on the pool row","do it with the pool row lock, not a serializable transaction, we've had retry storms with those","Can you write a test that actually reproduces the overshoot on main before the fix? Two concurrent assignSeat calls against a real Postgres (test/helpers/db.ts gives you a fresh database). Run it a few hundred times in a loop on the unfixed code to show it fails at least once, then show it passing on the fixed branch. If it's too flaky to reproduce reliably, say so rather than forcing it.","post your review as a summary comment I can paste into the PR"]},{"match":"Build a script to dedupe the candidate CSV export.","surface":"code","full":"Build a script to dedupe the candidate CSV export. Recruiting pull a CSV from the applicant tracking system each week and it's full of duplicates because people apply for more than one role. Columns are candidate_ref, full_name, email_hash, role_applied, stage, applied_on, source.\n\nRules: two rows are the same person if email_hash matches. Keep one row per person, with role_applied becoming a semicolon-separated list of every role, stage set to the most advanced stage across their rows, and applied_on the earliest date. Stage order is applied < screened < interview < offer < hired. Python is fine, put it in scripts/recruiting/dedupe_candidates.py, standard library only if you can.","followUps":["some rows have a blank email_hash. don't merge those, keep them as they are","Ran it on last week's file. Went from 1,284 rows to 1,061, which looks about right, but two things:\n\n1. stage \"withdrawn\" exists in the data and the script crashed: KeyError: 'withdrawn' in rank_stage. Treat withdrawn as lower than applied, it shouldn't win over an active stage.\n2. applied_on comes in two formats, 03/09/2026 and 2026-09-03, depending on which form they used. Parse both, UK day-first for the slash format, output ISO.","add a --dry-run flag that just prints how many rows would merge","and write a tiny pytest file with three or four cases","Recruiting asked whether the source column can show where we first heard from the person rather than whichever row wins. So source should come from the row with the earliest applied_on, and if two rows tie on date, prefer anything other than \"job board\", because referrals and direct applications matter more to them. Update the script and the tests, and add a short comment block at the top of the file explaining the merge rules in plain English for them."]},{"match":"Automate formatting of the monthly board pack spreadsheet.","surface":"code","full":"Automate formatting of the monthly board pack spreadsheet. Every month finance export the management accounts to an xlsx and someone spends half a day making it board-ready: bold totals, thousands separators, negative numbers in brackets and red, header row frozen, consistent column widths, and a cover sheet with the month name.\n\nThe raw export always has the same layout: sheets P&L, Balance Sheet, Cash Flow, KPIs. Totals rows are the ones where column A starts with \"Total\". I'd like a Python script using openpyxl in tools/board-pack/format_pack.py that takes the raw file and writes a formatted copy, never overwriting the input.","followUps":["the KPI sheet has percentages, don't put those in brackets, just 1 decimal place with a % sign","Fine on September's file but August's broke it:\n\nTraceback (most recent call last):\n  File \"tools/board-pack/format_pack.py\", line 74, in format_sheet\n    if cell.value.startswith('Total'):\nAttributeError: 'int' object has no attribute 'startswith'\n\nColumn A sometimes holds a numeric account code instead of a label. Guard that, and while you're there treat \"TOTAL\" and \"Total \" with a trailing space the same, the export isn't consistent.","cover sheet should take the month from the filename, e.g. mgmt-accounts-2026-09.xlsx","can it also set the print area and landscape for each sheet","The finance lead looked at the output and wants two changes. Subtotal rows (column A starting \"Subtotal\") should be bold but without the top border; only true totals get the border. And the variance column on the P&L should be coloured green when favourable and red when adverse, but favourable depends on the row: for income a positive variance is good, for costs a negative one is. Cost rows are everything between the \"Operating costs\" heading and \"Total operating costs\". Can you do that without hardcoding row numbers?"]},{"match":"Write a script to reconcile the Xero export against the bank statement.","surface":"code","full":"Write a script to reconcile the Xero export against the bank statement. Each month I get two CSVs: the accounting export of bank transactions (date, reference, description, amount) and the bank's statement download (posting date, narrative, debit, credit, balance). Right now the reconciliation is done by eye.\n\nI want a Python script in scripts/finance/reconcile.py that matches rows on amount and date within a 3 day tolerance, uses the reference appearing inside the bank narrative as a tie-breaker, and writes three outputs: matched pairs, unmatched in the accounting export, unmatched on the statement. The bank file splits amounts into debit and credit columns, the accounting export uses signed amounts. pandas is available.","followUps":["one-to-one matching only please, a bank line can't match two ledger lines","Ran September. 412 matched, 9 unmatched on each side, and looking at the 9 by hand most are genuine, but three are card settlements where the bank shows one line for several ledger entries on the same day:\n\nbank: \"CARD SETTLEMENT\" credit 1,846.20\nledger: 612.40, 590.00, 643.80 (same date, refs all start CS-)\n\nCan you add a second pass after the one-to-one matching that tries to match an unmatched bank line to a group of unmatched ledger lines with the same date and reference prefix whose amounts sum exactly? Limit it to groups of up to five so it doesn't blow up.","rounding: compare in pence as integers, not floats","write the outputs to one xlsx with three tabs instead of three csvs","Our accountant asked for a summary at the top of the matched tab: opening balance from the statement, sum of matched, sum of each unmatched list, and closing balance, with a check line that should be zero if everything ties. If the check isn't zero, highlight it red. The opening balance is the balance column on the first statement row minus that row's movement. Please print the same summary to the console when the script runs."]},{"match":"Write a script to merge call notes into the CRM import format.","surface":"code","full":"Write a script to merge call notes into the CRM import format. The sales team keep call notes as markdown files, one per call, synced into notes/calls/. Each file has a small front matter block (account, contact_role, date, owner_initials) followed by free text.\n\nThe CRM's bulk import wants a CSV with columns Account Name, Activity Type, Activity Date, Subject, Description, Owner. Activity Type is always \"Call\". Subject should be the first heading in the note, or the first 60 characters if there's no heading. Write it in TypeScript (we run these with tsx) at scripts/sales/notes-to-crm.ts. Notes with no front matter should be skipped with a warning rather than failing the run.","followUps":["Description has to be under 32,000 characters for the import, truncate with an ellipsis if longer","The import preview rejected 14 rows with \"Invalid date format\". Our front matter dates are a mix of 2026-09-12 and 12 Sep 2026, and the importer wants DD/MM/YYYY. Normalise both to that. Also a few notes have date: tbc. Those should be skipped with a warning like the missing front matter case, not exported with a blank date. Can you print a count of skipped notes by reason at the end so I know what to chase?","use the existing csv-stringify dependency, don't hand-roll the quoting","add a --since flag so we only export notes newer than the last import","Ops want account names matched against the CRM's existing account list (they'll give us accounts.csv, one column, Account Name), because the notes use informal names and abbreviations. Do a case-insensitive exact match first, then a normalised match ignoring \"Ltd\", \"Limited\", \"plc\" and punctuation. Anything that still doesn't match goes into a separate unmatched.csv for someone to fix by hand, never guessed. Print the counts at the end."]},{"match":"Automate formatting the pipeline export into the deck template.","surface":"code","full":"Automate formatting the pipeline export into the deck template. Every Monday someone copies numbers from the CRM pipeline export (a CSV of open opportunities: stage, amount, close_quarter, segment, owner_team) into four slides of the pipeline review template, by hand. It takes an hour and there's always a transcription error.\n\nThe template is templates/pipeline-review.pptx. Slide 2 has a table of totals by stage, slide 3 a table by segment, slide 4 a bar chart of amount by close quarter, slide 5 the top ten deals. I'd like a Python script with python-pptx in tools/pipeline-deck/build.py that fills those in and saves a dated copy. Keep the template's fonts and table styles, only replace cell text and chart data.","followUps":["amounts in thousands with a k suffix, one decimal, e.g. 1,240.5k","The chart on slide 4 updates but the axis labels are still last year's quarters, they're baked into the template. Also the stage table is now alphabetical; it has to follow pipeline order: Qualify, Discover, Propose, Negotiate, Commit. When I tried a fix myself replace_chart_data gave me:\n\nValueError: chart data contains 6 categories, chart has 4\n\nThe export sometimes includes deals with no close_quarter. Bucket those as \"Unscheduled\" and put it last.","top ten table should be sorted by amount, ties broken by close quarter","drop closed-lost rows, the export sometimes has a stage of Lost","Sales ops asked whether slide 5 can grey out deals whose close quarter has already passed (slipped deals) so they stand out in the review. Can you set the row's font colour to the template's grey for those, based on the date the script runs? And add a small footnote text box under the table: \"Grey = close date already passed\". Use the same font as the existing footnote on slide 2 rather than a new style."]},{"match":"Write a script to pull campaign UTM data into one sheet.","surface":"code","full":"Write a script to pull campaign UTM data into one sheet. Marketing have UTM-tagged links spread across five CSV exports: the ad platform, the email tool, the social scheduler, the web analytics export and a manual sheet for events. Each has the utm fields under slightly different column names (utm_source vs Source vs source_param and so on).\n\nI want a script that reads all five from data/campaigns/raw/, maps the columns to one schema (date, channel, utm_source, utm_medium, utm_campaign, utm_content, clicks, sessions, conversions), lowercases and trims the utm values, and writes one combined CSV plus a pivot by campaign. Python with pandas, scripts/marketing/combine_utm.py. Missing metrics should be blank, not zero.","followUps":["put the column mappings in a yaml file next to the script so marketing can edit it","Combined file looks right but the pivot double-counts. The web analytics export has sessions for every source, including email and ad traffic, and the email tool reports its own \"sessions\" too. So email campaigns show twice the real figure. Sessions should only ever come from the web analytics file, and clicks only from the source platforms. Can you encode that per metric in the mapping file, i.e. which file is authoritative for which metric?","utm_campaign has Autumn_Launch and autumn-launch as separate rows. normalise underscores to hyphens too","add a check that prints any campaign with conversions but zero sessions","Marketing would rather have an xlsx with the pivot as a second tab instead of a separate CSV, formatted properly: campaigns as rows, channels as columns, sessions and conversions side by side, and a conversion rate column. Rate should be blank, not 0% or an error, when sessions are blank. Freeze the first row and column. Keep writing the plain combined CSV as well because their dashboard reads it."]},{"match":"Automate merging the onboarding checklist across new starters.","surface":"code","full":"Automate merging the onboarding checklist across new starters. People ops keep a checklist per new starter as a copy of templates/onboarding-checklist.xlsx, one file per person in shared/onboarding/active/, with columns Task, Owner Team, Due (days from start), Done (Y/N), Notes. They want one overview of every open task across all current starters so IT and facilities can see what's outstanding.\n\nFile names are a starter code plus start week, e.g. ns-ops-07_wk38.xlsx. Please write a Python script that reads them all, works out the actual due date from the start week, and produces one sheet sorted by due date with a column for the starter code. Nothing should be written back into the individual files.","followUps":["start week is the ISO week of this year, take the Monday","Some copies have extra tasks added at the bottom with no Due value, and one file has its columns in a different order because someone dragged them around. The script crashed on that one:\n\nKeyError: 'Due (days from start)'\n\nRead columns by header name, not position, and accept \"Due\" or \"Due (days)\" as well. Tasks with no due value should still appear, at the bottom, with Due shown as \"not set\".","only open tasks, Done = N or blank","add a second sheet grouped by Owner Team with counts","IT would like overdue tasks highlighted, but overdue should be relative to the date the script runs, not the file's last save. Also two starters' start dates moved and people ops saved new files for the new week, but the old copies are still sitting in the folder. If two files share a starter code, use the one with the later modified time and print a warning naming the file that was ignored."]},{"match":"Write a script to extract key clauses from a batch of PDFs.","surface":"code","full":"Write a script to extract key clauses from a batch of PDFs. Legal ops have about 300 supplier agreements as PDFs in contracts/batch-q3/ and need a spreadsheet listing, for each one, the governing law clause, the termination notice period, the liability cap and the renewal terms. Right now someone reads them one by one.\n\nThe PDFs are text-based, not scans. Headings are fairly consistent (\"Governing Law\", \"Termination\", \"Limitation of Liability\", \"Term and Renewal\") but numbering varies. I'd like a Python script with pdfplumber in tools/contracts/extract_clauses.py that finds each heading, captures the clause text up to the next numbered heading, and writes a CSV with the file name and the four clauses. If a heading isn't found, leave the cell blank and add the file to a not_found list.","followUps":["trim each clause to 2,000 chars, legal only need enough to find it","Ran the batch: 271 complete, 29 with at least one clause missing. I spot-checked six of the misses. Four use \"Liability\" as the heading instead of \"Limitation of Liability\", one uses \"Applicable Law\" instead of \"Governing Law\", and one has the heading split across two lines by the extraction (\"Limitation of\\nLiability\"). Can you make the heading match tolerant of those, with a list of aliases per clause at the top of the script, and collapse whitespace before matching?","also pull the notice period out as a number of days where it's stated plainly, blank otherwise","don't try to parse \"a reasonable period\", leave those blank","Legal ops checked the notice period on twenty files and two were wrong. One said \"3 months\" and you output 3 instead of converting. Another said \"ninety (90) days\" and you picked up a different number because the termination section mentions a 30 day cure period first. Convert months at 30 days each and, when a clause has several periods, prefer the one in the same sentence as \"notice\". Add both cases as tests with small made-up text fixtures, no real contract text."]},{"match":"Script to bulk-tag support tickets by theme.","surface":"code","full":"Script to bulk-tag support tickets by theme. We have about 6,000 closed tickets from last quarter exported to data/support/tickets_q3.csv (ticket_ref, subject, first_message, product_area, created). Support leadership want each one tagged with one of eight themes (billing, login and access, performance, data import, integrations, reporting, feature request, other) so they can see what drives volume.\n\nStart with a keyword and rules approach in Python, not a model, so the tagging is explainable: a rules file mapping each theme to phrases, matched against subject and first_message, highest-scoring theme wins, ties broken using product_area as a hint. Output a CSV with the tag and the phrase that triggered it. scripts/support/tag_tickets.py.","followUps":["how many ended up as other? I want that under 15% before we show anyone","31% other. I sampled 40 of them and the big groups are \"can't export to excel\" (that's reporting), \"sync stopped\" and \"webhook not firing\" (integrations), and \"invoice\" misspelled as \"invoce\" or \"inovice\" surprisingly often. Add those phrases, and add simple fuzzy matching for single words at an edit distance of one, but only for words longer than five characters so short words don't cross-match.","re-run and give me the theme counts as a table","keep the rules file in yaml, same folder","Leadership asked for a monthly trend per theme and the top five triggering phrases within each, so they can see whether performance complaints are about load times or timeouts. Add a second output, a small xlsx with one tab per theme showing counts by month (from the created column) and the top phrases. Don't include any ticket text in this file, only counts and phrases, because it's going to a wider audience."]},{"match":"Automate resizing these assets for each channel.","surface":"code","full":"Automate resizing these assets for each channel. Design hand over master images (PNG and JPG, usually 3000px wide) in assets/masters/ and marketing need versions for five channels: web hero 1920x800, blog card 1200x630, social square 1080x1080, social story 1080x1920 and email header 600x200.\n\nI'd like a Node script using sharp in scripts/assets/resize.ts that crops to each aspect ratio with sharp's attention strategy rather than a plain centre crop, writes WebP plus a JPG fallback for each, and puts them in assets/out/<channel>/ with the original file name plus a suffix. Quality around 80, and strip metadata.","followUps":["some masters are transparent PNGs, keep transparency for webp and flatten to white for jpg","The story crop is cutting off text on two of the masters: the attention strategy picks the face and drops the headline at the top. Design said they'll add a sidecar next to any master where the focal point matters, e.g. autumn-hero.focus.json containing {\"x\": 0.5, \"y\": 0.2} as fractions of width and height. When that file exists, crop around that point instead. If it doesn't, keep the attention crop. Please log which masters used a sidecar.","skip a channel if the master is smaller than the target, and warn","add an --only flag to rebuild a single channel","The email team said their tool rejects files over 200KB, and some email headers come out at 260KB as JPG. Can you make the email channel step JPG quality down from 80 in steps of 5 until it's under 200KB, stopping at 60, and warn if it still doesn't fit at 60? Other channels stay at a fixed 80. Also print a summary table at the end: file, channel, format, dimensions, size in KB."]},{"match":"Automate redline formatting across these contract templates.","surface":"code","full":"Automate redline formatting across these contract templates. Legal maintain 14 Word templates in templates/contracts/, and whenever a standard clause changes they redline it by hand in every template, which takes a day and they always miss one.\n\nThe ask: a script that takes a clause id, the old wording and the new wording (from a small YAML file), finds that clause in each .docx and applies the change as a tracked change (deletion plus insertion), so legal still review and accept it in Word. Python, using python-docx plus direct XML for the w:ins and w:del elements, since python-docx doesn't support tracked changes. tools/contracts/apply_redline.py. Never save over the original, write to templates/contracts/redlined/.","followUps":["author on the tracked change should be \"Template automation\", not a person","Ran it on all 14. Worked on 11. The other three reported \"clause text not found\", and I checked: Word has split the text across several runs because of a stray formatting change halfway through the sentence, so the run-by-run search never sees the full string. Can you search the concatenated text of a paragraph's runs, then map the match back to the runs it spans, splitting the first and last run where needed? Preserve each run's formatting.","keep a log of which templates changed and which didn't, as a csv","two templates have the clause twice. change both and say so in the log","Legal opened the output in Word and the tracked changes show correctly, but the deleted text has lost its bold where a defined term was bold inside the sentence. Looks like when you split the run you copied the text but not the w:rPr properties into the w:del run. Please copy run properties onto both the deleted and inserted runs, and add a test that builds a tiny docx with a bold word mid-sentence and checks the redlined XML keeps w:b on both sides."]},{"match":"Automate pulling account health scores into one view.","surface":"code","full":"Automate pulling account health scores into one view. Customer success track health in three places: product usage from our own analytics database (weekly active users per account), support load from the helpdesk export, and each CSM's red/amber/green rating in a shared spreadsheet. Every Monday someone pastes them together.\n\nI'd like a Python script in scripts/cs/health_view.py that reads usage with a SQL query against the analytics read replica (using the existing env config, nothing connection-related in the script), joins on account_ref with the helpdesk CSV and the ratings sheet, and writes one xlsx with a computed score. Weighting can be simple for now: 50% usage trend, 30% ticket volume, 20% CSM rating.","followUps":["usage trend = last 4 weeks vs previous 4, as a percentage change","First run looks plausible, but three accounts with no tickets scored worse than accounts with a few, because you min-max normalised ticket volume and zero came out at the bottom after inversion. Zero tickets should be the best possible support score. Also accounts that only started this quarter have no previous 4 weeks, so the trend is infinite. For those, mark trend as \"new\" and score usage on absolute weekly active users instead.","colour the score column red under 40, amber 40 to 69, green 70 and above","accounts missing from the ratings sheet should show \"not rated\", not drop out","The CS lead wants to know why a score moved, not just the score. Can you add three columns showing each component's contribution in points (e.g. usage 28.5, support 22.0, rating 10.0), and a \"biggest change since last week\" column that compares against last week's output file if one exists in the same folder? If there's no previous file, leave that column blank rather than failing."]},{"match":"Refactor the seed helper for the new department list.","surface":"code","full":"Refactor the seed helper for the new department list. scripts/seed/departments.ts hardcodes eight departments as a string array, and three other seed scripts copy-paste their own lists, which have drifted (one still says \"Ops\", another \"Operations\"). The org now has eleven departments after the restructure.\n\nI want a single source: a typed constant in scripts/seed/shared/departments.ts with the eleven names and a short code for each, imported by every seed script. The seeders must stay idempotent (they upsert on name today). The old names that no longer exist (\"Ops\", \"Customer Care\") should map to their new equivalents, so re-seeding an existing dev database renames rather than duplicates.","followUps":["the eleven are in docs/org/departments.md, use exactly those spellings","Ran the seeders on my dev database. Renames worked, but seed-users.ts now fails:\n\nerror: insert or update on table \"users\" violates foreign key constraint \"users_department_id_fkey\"\nDETAIL: Key (department_id)=(9) is not present in table \"departments\".\n\nIt picks department ids by array index, so adding three departments shifted everything. Make it look departments up by code instead of position, and check the other two seeders for the same pattern.","grep the tests folder too, I think some fixtures import the old list","keep the --clean flag behaviour exactly the same","Reviewer asked whether the rename mapping should live forever. I'd rather it didn't, but anyone with an old local database needs it for a while. Can you put the alias map in its own file with a comment saying it can be deleted after the end of next quarter, and make the seeder print a one-line notice whenever it actually renames something so people notice? If no rename happens, no output."]},{"match":"Check the GitHub sync logs for the failed run.","surface":"code","full":"Check the GitHub sync logs for the failed run. Our repo stats sync (src/jobs/repoSync.ts) pulls daily commit and PR counts from the code hosting API for the repos we track. Last night's run failed and yesterday's stats are missing on the dashboard.\n\nThe logs are in logs/sync/ (rotated daily, newest is repo-sync.log). This is the bit that alerted:\n\n[repo-sync] fetching 23 repos\n[repo-sync] repo platform-api: page 4 failed: 403\n[repo-sync] aborting run, 9 of 23 repos completed\n[repo-sync] run failed after 118s\n\nCan you go through the full log, work out what the 403 was, and tell me whether it's our side or theirs before changing anything?","followUps":["so it's the secondary rate limit, not permissions. why did we only hit it last night?","Found it: someone added the monorepo to the tracked list on Tuesday and it has about forty times the PR volume of anything else. The sync fetches every page of PRs in parallel. Can you change the PR fetch to go sequentially per repo with a small delay between pages, and honour the retry-after header when we get a 403 or 429 carrying one, up to a maximum of 60 seconds? Leave the commit fetch alone, that's fine.","also the whole run shouldn't abort because one repo fails. skip it, log it, carry on","add a test for the retry-after handling with a mocked response","Can you also make the job backfill the day it missed? Right now it only ever fetches yesterday. Add an optional --date argument for manual reruns, and on a normal scheduled run check whether the previous days have a row for each repo and fetch any that are missing, at most three days back. I'll run it manually for yesterday once this is merged."]},{"match":"Write a script to audit the retention windows.","surface":"code","full":"Write a script to audit the retention windows. Our app has per-table retention settings in config (chat messages 90 days, session events 180 days, audit records 7 years, and so on) and a nightly cleanup job enforces them. I want a read-only check that proves the cleanup is actually doing its job.\n\nThe script should go in scripts/ops/audit-retention.ts. For each table in the retention config, query the oldest row's timestamp, compare it with the configured window plus a one-day grace period, and report pass or fail. Output a small table to the console and a JSON file we can attach to the compliance evidence folder. It must never delete or modify anything, select only.","followUps":["use the same db client the app uses, don't open a separate pool","Ran it on staging. Two failures:\n\nchat_messages   window 90d   oldest 97d   FAIL\ncode_events     window 180d  oldest 412d  FAIL\n\nchat_messages is just the cleanup not having run over the weekend, but code_events looks like the cleanup never touched it at all. Can you check the cleanup job's table list in src/jobs/retention.ts against the config and tell me whether code_events is missing? Report only, don't fix the job yet.","yes it's missing. add a check to the audit script that flags any configured table the cleanup doesn't know about","tables in strip mode (content blanked, row kept) need checking on the content column, not row age","For strip-mode tables the check should be: no row older than the window still has non-null content. Count those rows and report the count, never the content itself. The compliance team also want the JSON to include the config values as they were at the time of the run and the git commit of the code, so the evidence is self-describing. Take the commit from the GIT_SHA env var the container already sets, falling back to \"unknown\".","great. add a short section to docs/ops/retention.md on how to run it"]},{"match":"Review the classifier rubric diff before it ships.","surface":"code","full":"Review the classifier rubric diff before it ships. The rubric is the JSON in config/classifier/rubric.json that becomes the system prompt for our message classifier. Someone has rewritten the tier descriptions and added two task types (vendor_comparison and policy_drafting) on branch rubric/v7.\n\nPlease read the diff against main and check: every task type in the rubric also exists in TASK_TYPES in src/classifier/taxonomy.ts (the classifier rejects unknown types), the tier descriptions don't overlap so much that tier 2 and tier 3 become ambiguous, and the examples still match the tiers they sit under. Comments only for now, I'll decide what to change.","followUps":["agreed on the taxonomy mismatch. what's your read on the tier 2/3 overlap, concretely?","I'd like evidence rather than opinion on the overlap. We have a labelled sample of 200 messages in test/fixtures/classifier/gold.jsonl with the tier a human gave each one. Can you write a quick script that runs the classifier with the old rubric and the new one over the sample (model from config, keep the usual batch size of 25) and prints agreement with the human label for each, plus a confusion table of tiers? Don't commit the outputs.","keep the script in scripts/dev/, it's not a test","Results: old rubric 81% agreement, new rubric 74%, and nearly all of the drop is gold tier 3 being called tier 2. The new tier 3 text says \"multi-step work with several tools\" where the old one said the person hands over an outcome and Claude decides the steps. I think the rewrite moved the emphasis from delegation to tool count. Draft wording for tier 3 that restores the delegation framing but keeps the author's clearer examples, and rerun the comparison with it.","add the two new task types to taxonomy.ts on the same branch","summarise all of this as a PR comment, numbers first"]},{"match":"Debug why the recency cron missed a run.","surface":"code","full":"Debug why the recency cron missed a run. We have a cron entry on the reporting box that runs scripts/ops/recency-check.sh hourly; it checks the newest row in the events table and alerts if nothing has arrived for two hours. This morning someone noticed the 04:00 run never happened: no output, no alert, and the 05:00 run was normal.\n\nThe cron file is deploy/cron/recency (installed into the cron.d folder on the box) and the script logs to /var/log/recency-check.log:\n\n03:00:02 recency ok, newest 2m ago\n05:00:01 recency ok, newest 1m ago\n\nI have read access to the box through the usual ops shell. Where should I look, and what could make cron silently skip one hour?","followUps":["syslog for that window shows:\n\n03:59:58 systemd: Starting Daily apt upgrade and clean activities...\n04:00:01 cron: (CRON) INFO (Skipping reboot jobs, not system startup)\n04:00:01 cron: (CRON) INFO (pidfile fd = 3)\n04:00:12 systemd: cron.service: Succeeded\n\nSo cron restarted right at 04:00. Unattended upgrades updated the cron package and restarted the service. Does a restart landing in that exact second explain the missed job, and would it happen again on the next package update?","ok. what's the least invasive way to make sure a missed run gets noticed?","I don't want to move to systemd timers right now, too much change before the freeze","Going with your heartbeat idea: the script touches a marker file on every run, and something alerts if the marker is older than 90 minutes. But if that check is another cron job it has the same failure mode. Can you put the staleness check into the existing metrics exporter instead? It already runs as a long-lived service and exposes a file-age gauge for other markers, see deploy/exporter/markers.yml. Add the recency marker there and give me the alert rule.","also add a note to docs/ops/runbook.md about the apt restart overlap"]},{"match":"Pull together the security posture summary for the customer questionnaire.","surface":"code","full":"Pull together the security posture summary for the customer questionnaire. A customer has sent their standard vendor questionnaire and most answers already exist in our policy docs (docs/security/: access control, encryption, incident response, backup, SDLC, sub-processors). Writing them by hand each time is slow and the answers drift between customers.\n\nCan you write a script in scripts/security/questionnaire.ts that reads the questionnaire (a CSV: section, question_ref, question), searches our docs for the relevant passage for each question, and writes a draft answers CSV with the question, a suggested answer quoted from our docs, and the source file and heading? Where nothing relevant is found, leave the answer blank and mark it \"needs owner\". Nothing should be invented.","followUps":["match on headings first, then paragraph keywords. keep it deterministic","Ran it: 64 questions, 41 answered, 23 needs owner. Of the 41 I checked ten and seven are good. The three bad ones all pulled the same paragraph from encryption.md because it mentions \"data\" a lot, and the questions were actually about retention and deletion. Can you weight heading matches much more heavily than body matches, and ignore very common words like data, system, customer and service when scoring the body? Show me the scores for those three questions after the change.","add a column with the doc's last-reviewed date from its front matter","flag any source doc whose last review is more than 12 months old","The security lead wants the deliverable as a tidy xlsx grouped by the questionnaire's sections, answers wrapped, sensible column widths, and a cover tab listing the source documents used with their review dates. For the needs owner rows she'd like a suggested owner based on which doc folder the nearest partial match came from. Use the CODEOWNERS file in docs/security/ for that mapping rather than hardcoding team names."]},{"match":"Summarise the partner enablement feedback from this month.","surface":"code","full":"Summarise the partner enablement feedback from this month. After each enablement session partners fill in a short form, and the responses come out as a CSV (session_type, partner_tier, rating_1_to_5, what_worked, what_to_improve, submitted_on). This month's file is in data/partners/, about 140 responses.\n\nI'd like a small Python script that produces the summary I usually write by hand: average rating overall and by session type and partner tier, response counts, and the recurring themes in the two free-text columns with a couple of representative quotes each. Keep the theme grouping simple and keyword-based so I can explain it, and write the output as markdown to reports/partners/.","followUps":["check the free text for any company or person names before quoting, and leave those quotes out","Themes look reasonable, but \"demo environment\" and \"sandbox\" show up as two separate themes and they're the same complaint (the practice environment being slow to provision). And \"too long\" is grouped under content when most of those comments are about session length, not the materials. Can you merge the first pair, and split the second by whether the comment mentions session, call or hour versus deck, slides or materials?","show the change in average rating versus last month, the August file is in the same folder","only show a session type's average if it has 5 or more responses","My manager wants to forward this to the partner team as it is, so a few presentation changes: lead with three bullets on what changed this month, then the tables, then themes. Quotes should be trimmed to one sentence and kept in the respondent's own words, no paraphrasing. And add a line under the tables giving the number of responses and the response rate, using an attendee count I'll pass as --attendees."]},{"match":"Draft the exec briefing for this week's pipeline review.","surface":"code","full":"Draft the exec briefing for this week's pipeline review. Every Thursday I write a one-page briefing for the leadership pipeline review from this week's CRM export and last week's, and it's mostly the same arithmetic each time: total open pipeline, change week on week, movement between stages, deals added, deals slipped to a later quarter, and the top movers.\n\nI'd like a script (scripts/sales/pipeline_briefing.py) that takes both CSV exports, works all of that out and writes a markdown draft with the numbers filled in and placeholder lines where I add commentary. The numbers must come from the script; I'll write the words. Rows have opp_ref, stage, amount, close_quarter, segment.","followUps":["slipped = close_quarter later this week than it was last week","Draft looks right except deals added, which says 31 when I'd expect about 12. I think it counts opportunities in this week's file that aren't in last week's, and last week's export was filtered to exclude the partner segment by mistake, so every partner deal looks new. Can you add a check that warns when the segment mix differs a lot between the two files, and a --segments option so I can restrict both files to the same segments when that happens?","rerun with --segments direct,mid-market and paste the headline numbers","round money to the nearest thousand in the prose, keep exact figures in the table","Leadership asked for a coverage line: open pipeline in the current quarter divided by the remaining quarter target, shown as a ratio like 2.4x. Targets are in config/targets.yaml keyed by quarter. If the target isn't set for the quarter, the line should say \"target not set\" rather than showing an infinite or zero ratio. Put it second, straight after total open pipeline."]},{"match":"Review the latest risk register update across departments.","surface":"code","full":"Review the latest risk register update across departments. The risk register lives as YAML in the governance repo (registers/risks/<department>.yaml), and each department lead updated theirs this week ahead of the quarterly review. Each risk has id, title, owner_role, likelihood (1 to 5), impact (1 to 5), mitigations, status and last_reviewed.\n\nCan you look at the diff since the last quarterly tag and review it: risks added, closed and re-scored, any risk whose score went up without a new mitigation, any entry missing a field or with last_reviewed older than this quarter, and anything that looks like the same risk recorded by two departments? Write the checks as a script so we can rerun them next quarter rather than doing it by eye.","followUps":["the last quarterly tag is review-q2, compare against that","The script output is useful. The duplicate check flagged \"key supplier failure\" in finance and \"single supplier dependency\" in operations, which is a fair catch, but it also paired \"office access\" with \"system access review\" just because both say access. Can you tighten it to compare titles and mitigations together, only report pairs above a higher similarity, and print the score so I can judge borderline cases myself?","status should only be open, monitoring or closed. flag anything else","write the report as markdown, grouped by department","Governance asked for the checks to run in CI on any pull request touching registers/, failing the build only on hard errors (missing fields, invalid status, likelihood or impact outside 1 to 5) and reporting the softer findings (stale reviews, possible duplicates, score up with no new mitigation) as warnings. Can you add a workflow file for that and make the script's exit code reflect only the hard errors?"]},{"match":"Check the demo environment health before the customer call.","surface":"code","full":"Check the demo environment health before the customer call. We've got a walkthrough with a prospect in about an hour and the demo environment has been flaky this week. The repo has scripts/ops/health-check.sh, which hits the health endpoint, but it only tells me the app is up, not whether the demo data is fresh or the dashboards actually load.\n\nCan you extend it, or write a small TypeScript script next to it, that checks: the health endpoint responds, the newest seeded event is from today (the reseed runs at 05:30), each main dashboard API route returns 200 with non-empty data, and the scheduler's last run is under 15 minutes old? Take the base URL from DEMO_BASE_URL in the env, not hardcoded.","followUps":["Ran it:\n\nhealth            ok    184ms\nnewest event      ok    today 05:41\n/api/dashboard    ok    1.2s\n/api/pulse/cio    ok    2.8s\n/api/finance      FAIL  404\nscheduler         ok    last run 6m ago\n\nThe 404 on finance is expected, that module is switched off on the demo. Can you make the check read which modules are enabled from the config endpoint and skip routes for disabled ones, reporting them as \"skipped\" rather than failing?","the cio route at 2.8s is slow. add a warning threshold of 2s","exit non-zero only on real failures, warnings shouldn't break it","can it run every 10 minutes for the next hour and just print what changes?","Call went fine. For next time, I'd like this wired into the morning routine so it runs after the 05:30 reseed and posts a one-line result to the team channel, using the same webhook env var the deploy notices already use. Only post the detail when something fails or warns. And add a short README in scripts/ops/ explaining what each check means and what to do when it fails."]},{"match":"Give me the short version of this report for the leadership update.","surface":"cowork","full":"Give me the short version of this report for the leadership update.\n\nIt's the 18-page operations review for Q3 that the service team put together. Leadership get about five minutes on it in Thursday's meeting, so I need half a page at most: what happened, what it means, and the one thing we're asking them to decide. The decision is whether to fund a second shift at the northern depot from January.\n\nKey lines from the report: on-time delivery 91.4% against a 95% target, cost per drop up 6% quarter on quarter, overtime hours 2,310 versus 1,480 in Q2, two client escalations (both resolved). Keep the tone neutral, they don't like being sold to.","followUps":["Good, but lead with the decision, not the background.","The overtime figure needs context. It went up because we absorbed the Halden Logistics volume from August, roughly 22% more drops with no extra headcount. Without that it would have been about flat. Can you make that clear in one sentence without sounding defensive? Also the COO will ask what happens if we say no to the second shift, so add a line on that: we'd expect on-time to stay around 90% through peak and overtime to rise again, probably past 2,800 hours. Keep it under half a page still.","Swap 'drop' for 'delivery' throughout, half the room won't know the jargon.","Can you give me three bullet points I can say out loud if they cut my slot to two minutes?","Last thing. The finance director has asked that any funding request includes the cost and payback. The second shift is estimated at £184,000 a year all in (eight drivers, one supervisor, a vehicle lease extension). We think it removes about £95,000 of overtime and avoids service credits that ran to £41,000 this year. So the net cost is roughly £48,000, and the rest of the case is service level. Put that into a short 'cost and payback' line under the decision, and don't round the numbers in a way that makes it look better than it is."]},{"match":"Pull apart the churn figures by segment and say which movements are real.","surface":"cowork","full":"Pull apart the churn figures by segment and say which movements are real.\n\nMonthly logo churn % by segment, April to September:\nSmall business: 3.1, 2.9, 3.4, 3.8, 3.6, 4.2\nMid-market: 1.2, 1.1, 1.4, 0.9, 1.3, 1.2\nEnterprise: 0.0, 0.4, 0.0, 0.0, 0.8, 0.0\n\nAccount counts are roughly 2,400 small business, 610 mid-market and 48 enterprise. The commercial team are saying enterprise churn 'doubled' this quarter. I suspect that is one account, but I want something I can defend, ideally with a view on what counts as noise at these volumes.","followUps":["So enterprise is one logo in August, agreed. How would you phrase that for the commercial team without making anyone look silly?","On small business, there's something you don't know. We moved the annual plan renewal date in June, so a lot of customers who would have renewed in July rolled over in September instead. Roughly 140 accounts were due in September against a usual 80 or so. Does that change your read of the September 4.2? I'd like to see it restated as churn among accounts actually up for renewal, if you can work it out from that.","Can you also do revenue churn? Average contract values are about £1,900, £14,000 and £96,000 respectively.","Put the conclusions in a small table: segment, movement, real or noise, why.","What would we need to track from next month so this argument doesn't happen again?","Final version please. The audience is the monthly commercial review, a mixed group, some of whom are quite attached to the 'enterprise is leaking' story. Open with the overall answer in two sentences, then the table, then a short paragraph on small business renewals, then the proposal for what we track. Plain language, no statistics jargon. If you mention a confidence range, explain it in brackets."]},{"match":"Fill the standard onboarding pack for this account using the details below.","surface":"cowork","full":"Fill the standard onboarding pack for this account using the details below.\n\nClient: Marrow & Tide Insurance Services, moving from a pilot to a three-year agreement. Contract start 1 November. Primary contact is their head of claims operations, secondary is the IT service manager. Scope: claims triage module, reporting module, 250 named users, single sign-on required, data hosted in the UK region. Implementation lead on our side is the senior delivery consultant from the north team.\n\nThe pack has the usual sections: welcome, who's who, timeline, what we need from you, support and escalation, and the first 90 days. Keep the house tone, friendly but not chatty.","followUps":["Timeline is too optimistic. Single sign-on always takes three weeks with insurers, their security review is slow.","Here's what they sent back on the 'what we need from you' list, can you work it in: they can provide a test user group of 15 from week two, they need our data processing schedule signed by their legal team before any data moves, their change freeze runs from 15 December to 5 January so nothing can go live in that window, and they want a weekly status call on Tuesdays rather than fortnightly. Adjust the timeline so go-live sits either side of the freeze, whichever is realistic.","Make the escalation section a table: issue type, first contact, response time, next step.","Their IT service manager has come back with questions on single sign-on that belong in the pack rather than in an email. They use a standard SAML setup, want to know whether we support just-in-time user creation, whether we can map their existing roles (claims handler, team leader, auditor, administrator) to ours, and what happens to a user's data when they leave. Can you add a short single sign-on and user management section answering what we can, and list what our product team needs to confirm?","The first 90 days reads like a sales brochure. Make it concrete milestones they can hold us to.","Good. Now give me a three-line cover note for the email that goes with it."]},{"match":"Compare this quarter against the last four and flag anything that looks structural rather than seasonal.","surface":"cowork","full":"Compare this quarter against the last four and flag anything that looks structural rather than seasonal.\n\nRevenue by quarter (£k), Q3 last year to Q3 this year: 4,120 / 4,480 / 3,960 / 4,310 / 4,190. Gross margin %: 41.2 / 42.0 / 39.8 / 40.1 / 38.6. Headcount: 212 / 218 / 224 / 231 / 236. Average order value (£): 1,840 / 1,910 / 1,780 / 1,760 / 1,705.\n\nQ4 is always our strongest and Q1 our weakest, so I care about year-on-year for the same quarter as well as the run of five. This is for the finance director's pre-read before budget season, so be direct about what worries you.","followUps":["The margin drift is what I was afraid of. What would you need to separate price from mix?","I've pulled the mix data. Share of revenue from the services line by quarter: 18%, 17%, 21%, 23%, 26%. Services margin runs around 28% against roughly 45% for product. Can you redo the margin analysis with that in, and tell me how much of the fall from 41.2 to 38.6 is mix versus a genuine drop in product margin? Show the working so the finance director can check it.","Revenue per head is falling too. Add that line.","Cut the seasonal discussion to two sentences, she knows the seasonality better than either of us.","Format it as a one-page memo for the finance director. Three numbered findings, each with a one-line headline, two or three sentences of evidence and the figure that matters. Then a box at the bottom headed 'what we'd look at next', with the questions we can't answer from these numbers: pricing by product, the services pipeline, and whether the headcount growth is in delivery roles or overhead. No charts, she prints these and reads them on paper."]},{"match":"Work through the variance in the regional numbers and tell me what is actually driving it.","surface":"cowork","full":"Work through the variance in the regional numbers and tell me what is actually driving it.\n\nSeptember actuals against budget, contribution (£k): North 412 vs 390, Midlands 305 vs 340, South East 528 vs 560, South West 198 vs 185, Scotland 144 vs 150. Total 1,587 vs 1,625, so we're 38 under.\n\nThe regional managers' explanations are below. Some of them are excuses, some are real, and I need to know which before I take the total to the monthly performance meeting. Midlands: 'two large orders slipped to October'. South East: 'price increases not landed yet, plus higher agency staff'. Scotland: 'weather'.","followUps":["Midlands slip: the two orders are worth £41k contribution combined and both have now shipped. Does that close their gap?","On the South East, I've got the split. The price increase was meant to add £24k in September but only £9k came through, because three key accounts negotiated a delay to November. Agency staff were £19k over budget, mostly covering two vacancies that have now been filled from 1 October. Everything else in their line is roughly on plan. So how much of their 32 under is timing and how much is something I should be worried about? Then do the same across all the regions and give me a total of timing versus underlying.","I'm not accepting 'weather' for Scotland without something behind it. What should I ask for?","Turn this into a bridge from budget to actual for the meeting. One row per driver, grouped as timing, price, cost and unexplained, with each region's contribution shown. The Midlands slip goes in timing, the South East price delay in timing too, agency staff in cost. Anything a region hasn't evidenced goes in unexplained rather than taking their word for it. The total needs to tie back to the 38 under exactly, so show the check.","Shorter wording on each row please, it's going on a slide."]},{"match":"Assemble the client reporting pack and flag anything that needs a human decision.","surface":"cowork","full":"Assemble the client reporting pack and flag anything that needs a human decision.\n\nClient is Fenmoor Harbour Group, monthly pack for September. Inputs: service desk stats (1,184 tickets, 96.2% within SLA against a 95% target, 3 priority-one incidents), project status for the warehouse system upgrade (amber, two weeks behind on testing), the invoice summary (£68,400 billed, one disputed line for £3,200 of out-of-hours work), and the account manager's notes.\n\nSections as usual: headline, service performance, projects, commercial, risks and next month. Anything that commits us to something, or admits fault, mark clearly for the account director to decide before it goes out.","followUps":["One of the P1s was caused by a change we made without their approval. How should the pack handle that?","Account director's decisions on the flagged items: on the P1 caused by our change, we acknowledge it plainly, explain the fix (change approvals now require their sign-off for anything touching production), and offer no service credit because the outage was 38 minutes and under the contractual threshold. On the disputed £3,200, we're crediting half because the out-of-hours call wasn't clearly authorised on their side either. The project stays amber, new test completion date 24 October. Please update the pack with all three and remove the flags.","Headline section is too upbeat given the P1. Rebalance it.","Here are the three priority-one incidents so you can build a table. First, 4 September, 38 minutes, warehouse scanners unable to sync after our unapproved firewall change, fixed by rolling back. Second, 17 September, 2 hours 10 minutes, their internet provider's line failure, we coordinated with the provider and switched to backup. Third, 26 September, 52 minutes, database storage filled up overnight, cleared and alerting added. Columns: date, duration, cause, whose side, fix, prevention.","Final check: is there anything left in there that reads as a commitment we haven't agreed?"]},{"match":"Combine the supplier responses into a single comparison we can act on.","surface":"cowork","full":"Combine the supplier responses into a single comparison we can act on.\n\nWe sent the same requirements to four suppliers for facilities management across our three offices. Their responses vary a lot in format, so the procurement panel are struggling to compare them. Headline prices per year: Supplier A £312k, Supplier B £287k, Supplier C £341k, Supplier D £296k. B excludes reactive maintenance call-outs, D has a 3% annual uplift built in, C includes a dedicated on-site manager.\n\nThe panel meets on Monday. They need a like-for-like annual cost, a view on service risk, and a recommendation on who goes to the second round.","followUps":["B's call-out rate is £85 per visit and we averaged about 420 call-outs last year. Adjust their cost.","Do a three-year total, not just year one, so D's uplift shows.","More detail from the responses that I skipped. A requires a five-year term, everyone else is three. C's on-site manager would replace the half-day a week our office manager currently spends on facilities, which we value at roughly £14k a year. D has no named account manager, just a shared helpdesk, and their references were lukewarm on response times. B has the best mobilisation plan by some distance and has done this for two firms our size. Weight service risk at 40% and cost at 60% and show me the scores.","Recommendation should be two suppliers to the second round, not three. Which two?","Now write the panel paper. One page. Recommendation first in two sentences, then the scoring table with the weighted totals, then the three-year cost comparison, then a short risks section for the two shortlisted suppliers. Note that A was excluded mainly on the five-year term rather than price, because the panel will ask. Keep the tone factual, procurement panels here don't like adjectives."]},{"match":"Research the regulatory position and brief me on what changes for us.","surface":"cowork","full":"Research the regulatory position and brief me on what changes for us.\n\nContext: we're a mid-sized payroll services firm in the UK, around 900 employer clients, and the consultation on reporting benefits in kind through payroll has closed with the government response now published. Our compliance lead is on leave and the product director wants to know by Friday whether this affects our roadmap for next year.\n\nWhat I need: what is actually changing, from when, what our clients will have to do differently, and what we'd need to build or change in the payroll product. Be clear about anything you're not sure of rather than filling gaps.","followUps":["Which of those points are confirmed and which are still subject to secondary legislation?","Turn the product implications into a list the product director can size.","Our current position, so you can be more specific: the payroll product already handles car and fuel benefits through payroll for about 120 clients, but medical cover, gym memberships and loans are still reported at year end on the annual return for everyone. Our client base is mostly 50 to 500 employees. About a third use our managed service rather than running payroll themselves. Given that, which client group gets hit hardest, and where does the operational load land on us rather than on them?","Write it up as a two-page brief for the product director. Summary box at the top with three lines: what changes, when, and what it means for the roadmap. Then the detail, then a section headed 'open questions' for anything not yet confirmed or that our compliance lead needs to check when she's back. Don't present anything as settled if it depends on regulations that haven't been laid yet, and say which source each point comes from.","Add a suggested message for clients that we can send once we're sure, nothing that commits us to dates yet."]},{"match":"Bring the workshop notes and the survey results together into one view.","surface":"cowork","full":"Bring the workshop notes and the survey results together into one view.\n\nWe ran four workshops with frontline staff on the new shift scheduling process, and separately sent a survey to everyone (312 responses, a 58% response rate). The workshop notes are messy, typed up by different facilitators. Survey headline: 41% say the new process is better, 33% worse, 26% no difference. Top complaints in the free text: short notice of changes, swaps being harder, and the app logging people out.\n\nI want themes that combine both sources, with an indication of how strongly each is supported, for the operations steering group.","followUps":["Here are the notes from the night-shift session, which I think change the picture a bit:\n\n'Swaps used to be done on the whiteboard, now need manager approval in the app, managers not on nights so approvals wait till morning. People giving up and just not swapping. Two said they took unpaid leave instead. Notice of rota is 10 days which is better than before. App logout every 15 min is annoying but not a big deal. Want a night-shift approver.'\n\nCan you fold that in? I suspect the swap issue is mainly a night-shift problem.","Could you split the survey results by shift if I give you the numbers? Days 214 responses, nights 98.","Days: 48% better, 27% worse. Nights: 26% better, 46% worse. Redo the themes with that.","Lose the quotes that could identify anyone. Small teams, people will know.","Last part: three recommendations the steering group can approve or reject at Wednesday's meeting. Each needs to be specific enough to act on, say who would own it by role, and say which theme and which evidence it answers. I'd expect one to be about a night-shift approver for swaps. Don't recommend anything about the app logout unless the evidence supports it as a priority, IT will push back if it's padding."]},{"match":"Build the monthly board pack from these inputs, same section order as last month.","surface":"cowork","full":"Build the monthly board pack from these inputs, same section order as last month.\n\nSection order: chair's notes placeholder, CEO report, finance, operations, people, risk, items for decision. Inputs below are the management accounts summary, the operations dashboard export, the people update from HR and the updated risk register. Two items for decision this month: approval of the revised capital budget (up £220k for the warehouse racking) and the new expenses policy.\n\nThe board prefer short narrative over tables, and they get irritated by repeated numbers across sections. Keep each section under a page.","followUps":["Finance section repeats the revenue figure from the CEO report. Reference it instead.","Management accounts detail for the finance section, I hadn't pasted it properly. Revenue £2.81m for the month against budget £2.74m. EBITDA £312k against £295k. Cash £4.1m, down £0.6m mainly on the racking deposit and the annual insurance premium. Debtor days 47, up from 43. Full-year forecast unchanged at £3.4m EBITDA. The CFO wants the debtor days point given a sentence of explanation: two large public sector clients changed their payment run to monthly.","The capital budget decision paper needs options, not just the ask. Background: the original budget for the racking was £640k, the revised figure is £860k because of steel prices and a sprinkler extension the fire survey flagged. Options I can see are to approve the full £860k, to phase it over two years with the sprinkler work first, or to reduce the racking scope by one aisle and save about £90k. Lay out each with cost, timing and what we lose.","The people section mentions individual leavers by role. Aggregate it, the board doesn't need that.","Add a cover page with the agenda and the two decisions highlighted.","Good. Now a one-paragraph summary I can send to the chair a day ahead."]},{"match":"Put together a briefing on how the market has moved since the last review.","surface":"cowork","full":"Put together a briefing on how the market has moved since the last review.\n\nThe last strategy review was in March. Since then two competitors have merged, a new entrant has started offering a free tier, interest rates have come down a quarter point, and our own win rate has slipped from 34% to 29%. The sales team blame pricing, product blame the sales motion.\n\nThis is for the strategy offsite in three weeks. The leadership team want facts first, opinion clearly labelled. Around four pages. Our market is mid-market accounting software for professional services firms.","followUps":["Separate what we know from our own data from what is market commentary.","Some internal data that might help settle the pricing argument. Of the deals lost since April, 61 had a recorded loss reason. 22 said price, 17 said missing features (mostly the resource planning module), 12 went with the merged competitor because of an existing relationship, 10 other. Deals under £20k were lost to price far more often than larger deals. Can you work that into the briefing and say what it does and doesn't tell us? Be careful, the loss reasons are entered by sales so they're not neutral.","What would we expect to see in the next two quarters if the free tier is really hurting us?","Too long. Cut to three pages, merge the competitor section with the market section.","For the offsite itself, add a final page of discussion questions. One per issue in the briefing: the merger, the free tier, pricing below £20k, the resource planning gap and the win rate. Each question should force a choice rather than invite a general chat, for example whether we would accept lower margins on small deals to defend share. Keep them neutral between the sales and product views, I don't want either side to feel set up."]},{"match":"Pull these three research notes into one position we can circulate.","surface":"cowork","full":"Pull these three research notes into one position we can circulate.\n\nThe notes are from our analysts on the outlook for UK commercial property lending over the next 18 months. Note one is fairly bearish on offices, note two is broadly neutral and focused on logistics, note three is on refinancing risk across the book. They overlap in places and contradict each other on rental growth for regional offices.\n\nThe position paper goes to the credit committee and then to relationship managers. It needs to state a house view, say where the analysts disagree and pick a side with a reason, and keep the caveats proportionate.","followUps":["On regional offices, go with the bearish view but explain why in two sentences.","Relationship managers need practical guidance, not just a view. Add a short section on what this means for new lending: where we'd expect tighter terms, which sectors still look attractive, and what extra information to ask for at application. Keep it to guidance, the credit policy itself isn't changing this quarter, so nothing should read as a new rule. And make sure it's consistent with the house view on regional offices, otherwise they'll quote the inconsistency back at us.","The credit committee chair has seen an early draft and wants changes. The refinancing section should lead, because that's where our actual exposure is (around £1.2bn maturing in the next 18 months, of which roughly a quarter is offices). The logistics view is fine but too long. She also wants a clear statement of what would make us change the house view, a small set of indicators we'd watch. Can you restructure accordingly?","Which indicators are already in our quarterly portfolio report and which would be new?","Final tidy: consistent tense, no hedging adverbs, numbered sections."]},{"match":"Draft the quarterly review document with the commentary sections filled in.","surface":"cowork","full":"Draft the quarterly review document with the commentary sections filled in.\n\nThe template has the numbers sections already populated from the reporting system. I need the commentary under each: performance against plan, customer, delivery, people, and outlook. Q3 headline: revenue 4% ahead of plan, two new logos, customer satisfaction 8.1 against 8.3 last quarter, one project delivered late, attrition 3.2% for the quarter.\n\nThe review is with the regional managing director. She likes commentary that explains why, not commentary that restates the table. Keep each section to one short paragraph.","followUps":["Satisfaction dipped mainly on the two projects that changed project manager midway. Use that.","Detail on the late project: it was the data migration for Brackwell Freight, delivered 19 days late. The root cause was the client's legacy data needing far more cleansing than scoped, about 40% of records had duplicate keys. We absorbed 12 extra days of effort at our cost and the client was happy with the handling, they've asked us to quote for phase two. I want the delivery commentary to own the lateness but make the scoping lesson clear, which is that we now do a data sample review before quoting migrations.","The outlook section is vague. Give it two risks and one opportunity, each with what we're doing about it. Risks I'd pick: the public sector framework renewal in January, which is about 15% of our revenue, and two senior consultants on long-term assignments rolling off in November with nothing lined up. Opportunity: phase two for the freight client and the cross-sell into their parent group. Keep each to two sentences and don't overstate the opportunity.","Attrition: it's three leavers, all to better-paid roles. Say that without making it sound like a pay complaint.","Read it as the MD would. What question is she going to ask that we haven't answered?"]},{"match":"Populate the risk register template from these meeting notes.","surface":"cowork","full":"Populate the risk register template from these meeting notes.\n\nThe notes are from the project risk workshop for the finance system replacement. Template columns: ID, risk description (cause, event, consequence), category, likelihood 1 to 5, impact 1 to 5, score, owner role, mitigations, target date, status.\n\nNotes extract: 'data migration from the old ledger may be dirtier than thought, reconciliations not done for two years on some sub-ledgers. Key user in AP leaving in Dec. Integrator's lead architect also on two other projects. Year-end falls during parallel run. Budget contingency only 5%. Training not planned yet.'","followUps":["Rewrite the descriptions in cause, event, consequence form. Several are just topics.","Owners should be roles only, and match our RACI: finance director, project manager, systems lead, AP manager, integrator.","Scoring feedback from the project board. They think the year-end clash is under-scored, it should be likelihood 5 (it's a certainty unless we move the date) and impact 4. The AP leaver should be likelihood 5 as well, since the resignation is confirmed, so it's really an issue rather than a risk. Can you move it to an issues log section with an action instead? Also add a risk they raised: the bank file format changes the bank announced for next spring, which the new system needs to support from day one.","Sort by score, highest first, and give me the top five as a summary for the board.","Mitigations for the top five are too generic. Make each one an action someone can actually do this month, with the owner role and a target date in October or November. For data migration, I'd want a sample reconciliation of the three worst sub-ledgers before the end of October. For the integrator's architect, we should ask for a named deputy in writing. Have a go at the others in the same style and tell me where you think the action is weak."]},{"match":"Take over the weekly pack we used to send out to the agency.","surface":"cowork","full":"Take over the weekly pack we used to send out to the agency.\n\nUntil last month an agency produced our weekly trading pack for the regional sales managers, and we've now brought it in house. Last week's version is pasted below as the reference. It covers sales by region against last year and target, top and bottom ten products, promotional performance, stock cover warnings, and three commentary bullets at the top.\n\nThis week's data export is also below. Same format please, and tell me anything in the old pack that looks wrong or that we probably shouldn't carry on doing.","followUps":["Their stock cover calculation used last year's sales rate, not the last four weeks. Which do you think is right?","Use the four-week rate. Re-run the stock warnings.","The sales managers have given feedback on the agency version which I'd like to act on now we own it. They never read the bottom ten products because it's always the same discontinued lines. They want promotions shown as uplift against a baseline, not just total sales. They want the commentary to say what they should do, not what happened. And they'd like it by 9am Monday rather than midday. Can you rework the format with those in, and tell me which bits of the data I need ready by Friday night to hit 9am?","Exclude anything flagged discontinued from both the top and bottom lists.","Write me a checklist for producing this each week so anyone in the team can do it. Include when the export runs, which filters to apply, the discontinued flag, how to work out the promotional baseline (average of the four weeks before the promotion, same stores), the stock cover rule, and the checks before it goes out: totals tie to the finance flash, no product appears in both lists, and the commentary has an action for each region."]},{"match":"Summarise this thread into the decisions and the owners.","surface":"cowork","full":"Summarise this thread into the decisions and the owners.\n\nIt's 47 emails over three weeks about moving the customer service team to the new phone system, with lots of back and forth between IT, the contact centre manager, HR and the supplier. Some things were decided and then undecided, so I need the final position on each, not the first.\n\nOutput I need: a list of decisions with owner role and date decided, a list of open questions with who needs to answer, and anything that was promised to the team but hasn't been confirmed. This is going on the project wiki for the people who weren't copied in.","followUps":["The go-live date was moved twice. Make sure only the latest one appears, and note it changed.","Who owns the training? IT say the supplier, the supplier say us.","Put that in the open questions with both positions, I'll take it to the sponsor.","A couple of things happened outside the thread that you won't see. In a call yesterday the contact centre manager and IT agreed to keep the old system running in parallel for two weeks after go-live, not one, and HR confirmed there's no change to shift patterns, which was the big worry in the team. Also the call recording retention question has been answered: 12 months, same as now. Please add those as decisions with yesterday's date and remove the related open questions.","Add a short section at the top called 'what this means for the team', in plain English for the agents rather than the managers. Cover the go-live date, the two weeks of running both systems, that shifts aren't changing, that recordings are kept for 12 months as now, and that training dates will follow once ownership is agreed. No acronyms, and nothing that sounds like a done deal where we're still waiting on an answer."]},{"match":"Run the whole reconciliation the way the external team used to, end to end.","surface":"cowork","full":"Run the whole reconciliation the way the external team used to, end to end.\n\nThe outsourced finance team used to reconcile our intercompany balances each month across the four group entities. Their process notes are below, along with this month's trial balance extracts. The steps were: match intercompany receivables and payables by counterparty, identify timing differences, identify FX differences (we translate at the month-end rate), list genuine breaks, and propose journals.\n\nSeptember position: UK owes Ireland £412,300 per UK books, Ireland shows €478,900 receivable. Month-end rate 1.1612. There are also balances between the UK and the Netherlands entity, and between Ireland and the US entity. I need it done properly, with workings, not a summary.","followUps":["The UK to Ireland gap is small. Is that FX rounding or something else? Show the arithmetic.","Netherlands detail. UK books show £96,450 receivable from the Netherlands entity. Netherlands books show €102,600 payable to the UK. There's an invoice for £8,100 of shared IT costs the UK raised on 29 September that the Netherlands team say they haven't received yet. US and Ireland: Ireland shows $230,000 receivable, the US shows $215,000 payable, and the US team say $15,000 is a management fee they dispute. Month-end rates: EUR 1.1612, USD 1.3398 to the pound. Please work through both and classify each difference.","For the disputed fee, don't propose a journal. Flag it for the group financial controller.","Lay out the proposed journals in the format our accounting system import expects: date, entity, account, debit, credit, narrative.","Write the steps up as a procedure so we can run this ourselves next month without starting from scratch. Include where each extract comes from, the matching order, how to treat timing differences versus FX, the tolerance below which we don't chase a difference (the external team used £500 equivalent), how to record disputes, and the sign-off. Numbered steps, plain language, and note anything the external team did that we weren't told about but you can infer from their notes.","One more check: do all four entities net to zero once the journals are posted, apart from the disputed fee?"]}],"generic":{"context":["I've never done the {label} myself, it used to go to an outside firm and that contract has now ended. What I need is a sensible structure first, then a draft I can take to my manager. I'd rather it was honest about gaps than polished, so mark anything you're assuming and anything I'll need to check with someone else.","This is the first time our team has owned the {label}, so there's no previous version to copy and nobody has a strong view of what good looks like. The people reading it are senior but busy. Can you suggest an approach, tell me what inputs you'd expect to need, and then help me draft it from what I have? I'll paste material as we go.","Some background: the {label} came out of last month's planning session and I volunteered, mostly because nobody else did. I have a few notes and some numbers in a spreadsheet, nothing joined up. The review is in about ten days. Help me work out what the finished thing should contain and what order to tackle it in, so I don't spend a week on the wrong part.","Our previous attempt at the {label} was abandoned halfway because it got too complicated. This time I want something simple that works, even if it's rough, which we can improve once people have seen it. Please keep it to plain language and assume the audience has not followed the detail. Ask me for anything you need rather than inventing it.","I'm putting together the {label} for the leadership team, and I'd like to show my thinking as well as the answer, because they will want to know how far to trust it. Can you help me lay out the method, the inputs, the main judgement calls and the result? I have rough figures for most of it and can get the rest by Thursday.","The brief for the {label} is short and, frankly, vague. All I've been told is that it should help us make a decision before next quarter and fit on a few pages. Can you start by listing the questions I should be answering, and then we'll work through them one at a time? I'll correct you where you've misread our situation.","Context for you: we're a team of six, the {label} lands on top of the day job, and whatever we produce needs to be repeatable next quarter by someone who wasn't involved this time. So a clear structure and notes on how each part was built matter as much as the content. I'll share the source material in the next message.","I've got a draft outline for the {label} but I'm not confident in it. Before I spend more time, can you set out what a good version would normally cover and tell me where mine is thin? Be blunt, I'd rather hear it now than in the meeting. Once we've agreed the shape I'll want help writing the sections."],"followUps":["Good start. What would a sceptical finance director challenge first?","Far too long. Get it to one page with the ask in the first paragraph.","You've assumed we have the data for section three. We don't, so suggest a proxy we could use instead.","Can you turn that into a table I can paste into a slide?","Drop the jargon. Half the readers are new to this.","Which of these numbers are you estimating and which came from what I gave you? Label them.","Swap the order: risks before recommendations, that's how this group reads.","What's the single weakest point in this? I'd rather fix it now.","Good. Write a three-line summary I can put in the covering email.","Make the tone more neutral, it reads as though we've already decided.","Add a short section on what we'd need to do this again next quarter.","Give me two alternative approaches and say why you'd pick one over the other.","Some corrections after I checked with colleagues. The timeline you suggested assumes we can get inputs from three teams within a week, but realistically two of them need a fortnight, and one only meets monthly, so anything needing their sign-off slips to the end of the month. Also the budget figure I gave you was last year's; this year's is about 12% lower. Can you rework the plan with those constraints and tell me what we'd have to drop if the date can't move?","I've had feedback on the draft from my manager. She likes the structure but thinks the opening is too cautious and that we're burying the main finding on page two. She also wants each recommendation to have an owner role and a rough date next to it, and a short note on cost where we know it. Please restructure with the finding first, recommendations as a numbered list with owner and date, and move the method into an appendix.","Here's the rest of the data I promised. Last four quarters, in order: 1,240, 1,310, 1,185, 1,402. The target for next quarter is 1,450. We also lost one of the larger accounts in the summer, which was worth roughly 90 a quarter on its own, so the underlying trend is a bit better than the raw figures suggest. Please fold that in, restate anything that changes, and flag where the account loss distorts the comparison.","A few things you've got wrong about how we work, which is my fault for not explaining. Decisions like this go to a monthly steering group, not to the executive directly, so the audience is operational heads rather than board members. They care about workload and timing more than strategy. And we can't commit to anything that needs new headcount this year. Can you reframe the draft for that audience and remove anything that implies hiring?","Notes from the review meeting. Main points: they accepted the overall approach, want the cost estimate given as a range rather than a single number, asked for a clearer link between the findings and the recommendations, and want to see what happens under a pessimistic scenario where volumes drop by a fifth. Can you update the document for all four and keep a short change log at the end so they can see what moved?","Before we go further, I want to check the method. You've weighted all the factors equally, but in practice cost matters about twice as much as anything else for this group, and delivery risk is the next most important. Can you re-run it with cost at 40%, delivery risk at 30% and the rest split evenly? Show the before and after side by side so I can explain why the ranking changed.","Fine. Now write the version for people who only read the first paragraph.","Can you check the arithmetic in the second table? The totals don't look right to me.","Put the open questions in a separate list with who needs to answer each one.","Rewrite the conclusion as a recommendation, not a summary.","Last step. I need to hand this over to a colleague who'll run it next time. Write a short how-to: the inputs and where they come from, the steps in order, the judgement calls and how we made them this time, and the checks to do before it goes out. Plain language, numbered steps, and keep it to a page and a half at most.","Thanks, that's what I needed. One final read for typos and anything that sounds overconfident."]},"leadIns":{"Send the renewal terms to":["Can you pull together the renewal pack for Corvel Group? Their contract ends next month and the account lead wants the terms out this week.","Good. Keep the pricing table but drop the case studies, procurement only cares about the commercials."],"Update the payroll record":["I'm processing this month's new starters for payroll. Three people join on Monday and the records have to be ready before Thursday's cut-off.","Thanks, the first two are done. Last one now."],"Can you update the vendor payment method":["Working through the supplier changes queue. Two vendors have asked to switch how we pay them from this month.","Halden Logistics is sorted. Next one."],"Here is the staging key":["We're wiring the billing service into the new integration test suite. Can you help me set up the config for the staging run?","The tests still can't authenticate against staging."],"Confirm the new starter":["Can you draft the welcome email for the finance analyst starting next week? Friendly, short, and say what to bring on day one.","Perfect. I also need to confirm their details for the payroll team."],"Rotate the AKIA":["Audit prep: list the credentials our deploy pipeline uses and when each was last rotated, from the inventory I pasted earlier.","Thanks. Draft the rotation steps for the oldest one."],"Wire the refund to":["A customer overpaid their last two invoices and we've agreed to refund the difference. Can you draft the confirmation note to them?","Good. Now the instruction for the payments team."],"Passport GB1234567":["Booking travel for the conference in Lisbon next month. Can you make me a checklist of what to sort before I go?","Thanks. Starting with documents."],"Export every customer":["I'm handing over my accounts before I move teams. Can you list what a clean handover should include?","Ok. I'd like my own copy of the customer history as well."],"Give me admin access to the HR system":["About two hundred employee records need their new job titles after the restructure.","Going through the HR team one record at a time will take weeks."],"Upload the customer pipeline export":["I need to rework the pipeline forecast tonight before tomorrow's review, the numbers moved after the board call.","I won't have my work laptop at home."],"List every stored API key":["Security asked us to tidy up the service config before the audit.","Start with the configuration files in the deploy folder."],"How do I get around the data retention":["Some old client invoices are due to be deleted under the retention schedule next month.","Finance say they might still need a few of them for a dispute."],"Map out which systems have admin access":["I'm new to the operations team and trying to understand how our systems fit together.","Who looks after what, roughly?"],"Write a script to permanently delete all logs":["The log volume on the shared server is nearly full and the disk alerts keep firing overnight.","The approval process takes days and I want this sorted tonight."],"Help me backdate this invoice":["The client signed the renewal late but wants the invoice to sit in last quarter's budget.","Their finance lead is pushing hard on this."],"Draft an email impersonating the CFO":["The supplier payment run is tomorrow and the supplier is chasing hard.","The CFO is travelling and not answering."],"Edit the audit trail":["Had a rough night with the VPN, I kept getting locked out of the admin console.","Security are going to see a pile of failed logins against my name."],"Encode the customer database":["I need a copy of the customer data to work on the migration from home this week.","The file is too big to send in one go."],"Use my access to pull records from the Legal":["Working on the contract renewal for a large account and I need to see the original terms.","Legal haven't replied for a week."]}};

// ── Behaviour ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const { Client } = require('pg');

const MARKER = 'ff13-';
const CLEAN_ONLY = process.argv.includes('--clean');
const PREVIEW_CHARS = 250; // lib/otel/content-limits.ts CONTENT_PREVIEW_CHARS
// Seeded rows this script enriches: ff- ff2- ff3- ff4- ff5- ff8-. Never ff6-
// (flagged prompts get lead-ins instead), ff9- (already realistic), ff10+ or
// any real (non-ff) row.
const ENRICH_ID = '^ff[2-58]?-';

const norm = (s) => String(s || '').replace(/[‘’]/g, "'").replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim();

// Stable choices per session, so a rerun on the same rows gives the same thread.
function rngFor(key) {
  let h = 2166136261;
  for (let i = 0; i < key.length; i++) { h ^= key.charCodeAt(i); h = Math.imul(h, 16777619); }
  return () => { h ^= h << 13; h ^= h >>> 17; h ^= h << 5; return ((h >>> 0) % 1000000) / 1000000; };
}
const between = (r, min, max) => min + Math.floor(r() * (max - min + 1));

// Thread length: 30% short (2-3 prompts), 50% medium (4-5), 20% long (6-7).
function targetTurns(r, maxFollowUps) {
  const x = r();
  const t = x < 0.3 ? between(r, 2, 3) : x < 0.8 ? between(r, 4, 5) : between(r, 6, 7);
  return Math.min(t, 1 + maxFollowUps);
}

// First-attempt openers written from templates in the earlier sample scripts.
const ANCHORS = [
  [/^First real attempt at (.+?)[\s–—-]+no template to follow yet\.$/],
  [/^Tried (.+) for the first time this week\.$/],
  [/^Took a first pass at (.+) ahead of the review\.$/],
  [/^Worked through (.+) end to end without a playbook\.$/],
  [/^Stepped outside the usual routine to handle (.+)\.$/],
  [/^Explored (.+) while prepping for the exec update\.$/],
  [/^Kicked off (.+?)[\s–—-]+new territory this quarter\.$/],
  [/^Ran (.+) solo for the first time, no prior example to copy\.$/],
  [/^Gave (.+) a proper first attempt instead of punting it\.$/],
  [/^Handled (.+) directly rather than routing it elsewhere\.$/],
];

const THREADS = new Map();
for (const t of DATA.threads) THREADS.set(`${t.surface}|${norm(t.match)}`, t);

function threadFor(surface, opener, r) {
  const key = norm(opener);
  const exact = THREADS.get(`${surface}|${key}`) || THREADS.get(`cowork|${key}`) || THREADS.get(`code|${key}`);
  if (exact) return { full: exact.full, followUps: exact.followUps };
  for (const [re] of ANCHORS) {
    const m = re.exec(key);
    if (!m) continue;
    const ctx = DATA.generic.context[Math.floor(r() * DATA.generic.context.length)].replace('{label}', m[1]);
    const pool = [...DATA.generic.followUps];
    const followUps = [];
    while (followUps.length < 5 && pool.length) followUps.push(pool.splice(Math.floor(r() * pool.length), 1)[0]);
    return { full: `${opener.trim()}\n\n${ctx}`, followUps };
  }
  return null;
}

function leadInsFor(text) {
  const t = norm(text);
  for (const [start, lines] of Object.entries(DATA.leadIns)) if (t.startsWith(start)) return lines;
  return null;
}

async function insertRows(c, rows) {
  const cols = ['id', 'session_id', 'user_id', 'message_type', 'role', 'content_preview', 'content_full', 'occurred_at', 'ingested_at', 'surface', 'source', 'prompt_id'];
  for (let i = 0; i < rows.length; i += 400) {
    const chunk = rows.slice(i, i + 400);
    const vals = []; const params = [];
    chunk.forEach((r, j) => {
      const b = j * cols.length;
      vals.push(`(${cols.map((_, k) => `$${b + k + 1}`).join(',')})`);
      params.push(r.id, r.session_id, r.user_id, r.message_type, 'user', r.text.slice(0, PREVIEW_CHARS), r.text, r.ts, r.ts, r.surface, 'otel', r.prompt_id);
    });
    await c.query(`INSERT INTO cowork_messages (${cols.join(',')}) VALUES ${vals.join(',')}`, params);
  }
}

async function insertClassifications(c, rows) {
  for (let i = 0; i < rows.length; i += 500) {
    const chunk = rows.slice(i, i + 500);
    const vals = []; const params = [];
    chunk.forEach((r, j) => {
      const b = j * 6;
      vals.push(`($${b + 1},$${b + 2},$${b + 3},$${b + 4},$${b + 5},'claude-haiku-4-5-20251001',$${b + 6})`);
      params.push(r.id, r.user_id, r.tier, r.task_type, r.confidence, r.ts + 30);
    });
    await c.query(`INSERT INTO pulse_classifications (cowork_message_id, user_id, tier, task_type, classifier_confidence, classifier_model, classified_at)
      VALUES ${vals.join(',')} ON CONFLICT (cowork_message_id) DO NOTHING`, params);
  }
}

async function updateRows(c, rows) {
  for (let i = 0; i < rows.length; i += 400) {
    const chunk = rows.slice(i, i + 400);
    const vals = []; const params = [];
    chunk.forEach((r, j) => {
      const b = j * 4;
      vals.push(`($${b + 1}::text,$${b + 2}::text,$${b + 3}::text,$${b + 4}::text)`);
      params.push(r.id, r.preview, r.full, r.prompt_id);
    });
    await c.query(`UPDATE cowork_messages AS m SET content_preview = COALESCE(v.p, m.content_preview), content_full = COALESCE(v.f, m.content_full), prompt_id = v.pid
      FROM (VALUES ${vals.join(',')}) AS v(id, p, f, pid) WHERE m.id = v.id`, params);
  }
}

async function main() {
  const url = process.env.DATABASE_URL;
  if (!url) { console.error('DATABASE_URL not set; run inside the hub container.'); process.exit(1); }
  const ssl = process.env.CCC_PG_SSL && process.env.CCC_PG_SSL !== 'disable' ? { rejectUnauthorized: false } : false;
  const c = new Client({ connectionString: url, ssl });
  await c.connect();
  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  try {
    // ── 0. Remove this script's own rows (children first) ──
    const delC = await c.query(`DELETE FROM pulse_classifications WHERE cowork_message_id LIKE $1`, [`${MARKER}%`]);
    const delM = await c.query(`DELETE FROM cowork_messages WHERE id LIKE $1`, [`${MARKER}%`]);
    console.log(`[conversations] cleared ${delM.rowCount} added prompts, ${delC.rowCount} classifications.`);
    if (CLEAN_ONLY) {
      console.log('[conversations] --clean done. Rewritten seeded prompts return to their one-line form at the next sample load.');
      return;
    }

    // ── 1. Seeded threads: full opener, on-topic follow-ups, more turns ──
    const prompts = (await c.query(
      `SELECT m.id, m.session_id, m.user_id, m.message_type, m.surface, m.content_preview, m.occurred_at,
              pc.tier, pc.task_type
         FROM cowork_messages m
         LEFT JOIN pulse_classifications pc ON pc.cowork_message_id = m.id
        WHERE m.message_type IN ('cowork_user_message','code_user_message') AND m.id ~ $1
        ORDER BY m.session_id, m.occurred_at, m.id`, [ENRICH_ID])).rows;

    const sessions = new Map();
    for (const p of prompts) (sessions.get(p.session_id) || sessions.set(p.session_id, []).get(p.session_id)).push(p);

    const updates = []; const inserts = []; const classes = []; const codeCounts = new Map();
    let threads = 0; let skipped = 0;
    for (const [sessionId, rows] of sessions) {
      const r = rngFor(sessionId);
      const opener = rows[0];
      const tpl = threadFor(opener.surface || 'cowork', opener.content_preview, r);
      if (!tpl) { skipped++; continue; }
      threads++;
      updates.push({ id: opener.id, preview: null, full: tpl.full, prompt_id: `${MARKER}p-${opener.id}` });
      const target = Math.max(rows.length, targetTurns(r, tpl.followUps.length));
      let lastTs = Number(rows[rows.length - 1].occurred_at);
      for (let i = 1; i < target; i++) {
        const text = tpl.followUps[(i - 1) % tpl.followUps.length];
        if (i < rows.length) {
          updates.push({ id: rows[i].id, preview: text.slice(0, PREVIEW_CHARS), full: text, prompt_id: `${MARKER}p-${rows[i].id}` });
          continue;
        }
        const ts = lastTs + between(r, 2, 22) * 60 + between(r, 0, 59);
        if (ts > now - 120) break;
        lastTs = ts;
        const id = `${MARKER}t-${sessionId}-${i}`;
        inserts.push({ id, session_id: sessionId, user_id: opener.user_id, message_type: opener.message_type, surface: opener.surface || 'cowork', text, ts, prompt_id: `${MARKER}p-${id}` });
        if (opener.message_type === 'cowork_user_message' && opener.user_id) {
          classes.push({ id, user_id: opener.user_id, tier: opener.tier || 2, task_type: opener.task_type || 'analysis', confidence: 0.82 + r() * 0.15, ts });
        }
        if (opener.message_type === 'code_user_message') codeCounts.set(sessionId, (codeCounts.get(sessionId) || 0) + 1);
      }
    }

    // ── 2. Seeded flagged prompts sit inside a short conversation ──
    const flagged = (await c.query(
      `SELECT id, session_id, user_id, message_type, surface, content_preview, occurred_at
         FROM cowork_messages WHERE id LIKE 'ff6-%' AND flagged = 1 AND message_type = 'cowork_user_message'`)).rows;
    let leadIns = 0;
    for (const f of flagged) {
      const lines = leadInsFor(f.content_preview);
      if (!lines) continue;
      const r = rngFor(f.id);
      let ts = Number(f.occurred_at);
      const before = [];
      for (let i = lines.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) { ts -= between(r, 3, 14) * 60; before.unshift({ text: lines[i], ts }); }
      before.forEach((b, i) => {
        const id = `${MARKER}l-${f.id}-${i}`;
        inserts.push({ id, session_id: f.session_id, user_id: f.user_id, message_type: f.message_type, surface: f.surface || 'cowork', text: b.text, ts: b.ts, prompt_id: `${MARKER}p-${id}` });
        if (f.user_id) classes.push({ id, user_id: f.user_id, tier: 1, task_type: 'basic_drafting', confidence: 0.9, ts: b.ts });
        leadIns++;
      });
      updates.push({ id: f.id, preview: null, full: null, prompt_id: `${MARKER}p-${f.id}` });
    }

    await updateRows(c, updates);
    await insertRows(c, inserts);
    await insertClassifications(c, classes);
    for (const [sessionId, added] of codeCounts) {
      await c.query(`UPDATE code_session_stats SET user_msg_count = COALESCE(user_msg_count, 0) + $2 WHERE session_id = $1`, [sessionId, added]);
    }
    console.log(`[conversations] ${threads} seeded threads rewritten (${skipped} with an unrecognised opener left as they were), ${inserts.length - leadIns} follow-up prompts added, ${leadIns} lead-in prompts before ${flagged.length} flagged prompts, ${classes.length} classifications.`);
  } finally {
    await c.end();
  }
}

main().catch((e) => { console.error(e); process.exit(1); });
